arXiv:2107.03918v5 [math.AG] 04 Apr 2024</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno" lang="en">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The moduli stack of principal <math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tomás L. Gómez
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text">Instituto de Ciencias Matemáticas (CSIC-UAM-UC3M-UCM),
Nicolás Cabrera 15, Campus Cantoblanco UAM, 28049 Madrid, Spain</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text">tomas.gomez@icmat.es</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Andres Fernandez Herrero
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span><span id="id3" class="ltx_text">Mathematics Hall MC 4404, 2990 Broadway,
New York, N.Y. 10027, USA.</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text">af3358@columbia.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alfonso Zamora
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span><span id="id5" class="ltx_text">Departamento de Matemática Aplicada a las TIC, ETSI Informáticos, Universidad Politécnica de Madrid, Campus de Montegancedo,
28660 Madrid, Spain</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id6" class="ltx_text">alfonso.zamora@upm.es</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth projective variety and let <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected reductive group, both defined over a field of characteristic <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a faithful representation <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> into a product of general linear groups, we define a moduli stack of principal <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves that compactifies the stack of <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles on <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. We apply the theory developed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AHLH23</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>]</cite> to construct a moduli space of Gieseker semistable principal <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves. This provides an intrinsic stack-theoretic construction of the moduli space of semistable singular principal bundles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch02</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">GLSS08</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Our second main result is the definition of a schematic Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtration for <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves, which induces a stratification of the stack by locally closed substacks. This filtration for a general reductive group <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a refinement of the canonical slope parabolic reduction previously considered at the level of points in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AAB02</a>]</cite> and as a stratification of the stack in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">GN18</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">GN16</a>]</cite>. In an appendix, we apply the same techniques to define Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations in arbitrary characteristic and show that they induce a stratification of the stack by radicial morphisms.</span></p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6><span id="id7" class="ltx_text">Moduli spaces, principal bundles, stacks, <math id="m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-stratifications, Harder-Narasimhan filtration, Gieseker stability, principal <math id="m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2020 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6><span id="id8" class="ltx_text">14D20, 14D23, 14F06, 14J60, 14L24</span>
</div>
