<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix a smooth projective variety <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and a connected reductive group <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> defined over a field of characteristic <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations in positive characteristic ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for positive characteristic). When <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X=1</annotation></semantics></math>, Ramanathan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ram75</a>]</cite> constructed a projective moduli space of semistable
principal <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles on <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> using Geometric Invariant Theory (GIT). The stability condition in this case is analogous to the notion of slope stability for a vector bundle (introduced by Mumford), in the sense that it involves the degree and the rank of certain vector bundles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, it is necessary to consider also degenerations of principal <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles in order to construct a proper moduli space. These degenerations are called principal <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves, where <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is a choice of representation of <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. A principal <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf is a triple <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P,\mathcal{E},\psi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P,\mathcal{E},\psi)</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of a torsion-free sheaf <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, a principal <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> defined on the open set <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally free, and an isomorphism <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> between the restriction <math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and the vector bundle associated to <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> using the representation <math id="S1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> (see Definition <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Principal 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves and the moduli stack 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                Bun
                ρ
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  X
                  ) ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm17" title="Proposition 2.17. ‣ 2.3. The scheme of 
          
            G
          
        -reductions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.17</span></a>). More generally, we allow <math id="S1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> to be a faithful homomorphism from <math id="S1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> into a product of general linear groups (see Definition <a href="#S6.Thmthm1" title="Definition 6.1. ‣ 6.1. Generalization: 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves for products of general linear groups ‣ 6. Generalization to products of general linear groups and comparison with previous work ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>, Definition <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Definition 6.3. ‣ 6.2. Comparison with previous work ‣ 6. Generalization to products of general linear groups and comparison with previous work ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>, and Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Generalization to products of general linear groups and comparison with previous work ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>)<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          If we want our definition of stability to coincide with Ramanathan’s Definition <a href="#S6.Thmthm5" title="Definition 6.5 ([]). ‣ 6.2. Comparison with previous work ‣ 6. Generalization to products of general linear groups and comparison with previous work ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a> in the case of curves, then we have to require that the representation <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is central (Definition <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Definition 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Notation ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>).</span></span></span>.
These <math id="S1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves have also been called in the literature “singular principal bundles” by Schmitt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch02</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">GLSS08</a>]</cite> (in the case when <math id="S1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is valued in a special linear group), and “principal <math id="S1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves” by Gómez-Sols <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">GS05</a>]</cite> (when <math id="S1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is the adjoint representation, not injective in general). We show that there is an algebraic stack <math id="S1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Bun</mtext><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> that parametrizes principal <math id="S1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves on <math id="S1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When going from <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, an additional consideration needs to be introduced in order to construct the moduli space. Instead of using slope semistability, we have to use a notion where the degree is substituted by the Hilbert polynomial. This is Gieseker semistability for torsion-free sheaves. An analogous notion of semistability was defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">GS05</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">GLSS08</a>]</cite> for principal <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves. Using the methods of GIT, Gómez-Sols <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">GS05</a>]</cite>, Schmitt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch02</a>]</cite> and Gómez-Langer-Schmitt-Sols <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">GLSS08</a>]</cite> constructed moduli spaces of semistable <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves. We remark that the moduli space obtained will in general depend on the choice of representation <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
The notion of semistability for principal <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves is given in Definition <a href="#S4.Thmthm28" title="Definition 4.28. ‣ 4.5. 
          
            Θ
          
        -stratification, semistability and leading term filtrations ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.28</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Alper, Halpern-Leistner and Heinloth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AHLH23</a>]</cite> have recently introduced an alternative approach to GIT for the construction of moduli spaces. Their approach is intrinsic to the algebraic stack associated to the moduli problem of interest. Building on the criteria of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AHLH23</a>]</cite>, Halpern-Leistner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>, §5]</cite> and Halpern-Leistner, Herrero and Jones <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>]</cite> developed tools to construct moduli spaces of semistable loci inside of algebraic stacks. In this article we employ their techniques instead of the GIT methods that were previously used in the literature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this direction, our first main result in this article is an intrinsic-stack theoretic construction of the moduli space of semistable <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves, where <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is any faithful representation of <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> into a product of general linear groups. In order to obtain a quasicompact moduli space, it is helpful to fix a tuple <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\bullet}=(P^{1},P^{2},\ldots,P^{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\bullet}=(P^{1},P^{2},\ldots,P^{b})</annotation></semantics></math> of rational polynomials <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{i}\in\mathbb{Q}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{i}\in\mathbb{Q}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>
and restricts to the locus of <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves where the <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> underlying sheaf has Hilbert polynomial <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We state the result here in the case when the representation <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is central (Definition <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Definition 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Notation ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>)<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          A more general modified statement is proven in the body of the document, see Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 6.2. ‣ 6.1. Generalization: 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves for products of general linear groups ‣ 6. Generalization to products of general linear groups and comparison with previous work ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>.</span></span></span>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a central faithful representation <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:G\hookrightarrow\prod_{i}\GL_{n_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:G\hookrightarrow\prod_{i}\GL_{n_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The moduli stack </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Bun</mtext><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-sheaves admits a </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-stratification induced by the polynomial numerical invariant </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> defined in Subsection </span><a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. The numerical invariant ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Semistable reduction) The semistable locus </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)^{ss}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Bun</mtext><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)^{ss}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfies the existence part of the valuative criterion for properness.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any choice of tuple of rational polynomials </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the open substack </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)^{ss,P^{\bullet}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Bun</mtext><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)^{ss,P^{\bullet}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-sheaves with tuple </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as Hilbert polynomials admits a proper good moduli space (in the sense of </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alp13</a><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (a) is proven in subsection <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. 
          
            Θ
          
        -stratification, semistability and leading term filtrations ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm27" title="Theorem 4.27. ‣ 4.5. 
          
            Θ
          
        -stratification, semistability and leading term filtrations ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.27</span></a>), (b) is proven in subsection <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1. Completeness of the stack 
          
            
              
                
                Bun
                ρ
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  X
                  ) ‣ 5. Moduli space for the semistable locus ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> (Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 5.8 (Semistable Reduction). ‣ 5.1. Completeness of the stack 
          
            
              
                
                Bun
                ρ
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  X
                  ) ‣ 5. Moduli space for the semistable locus ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>) and (c) is proven in subsection <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Boundedness and moduli space for the semistable locus ‣ 5. Moduli space for the semistable locus ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> (Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm16" title="Theorem 5.16. ‣ 5.3. Boundedness and moduli space for the semistable locus ‣ 5. Moduli space for the semistable locus ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">If the representation <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is valued in a single special linear group (resp. <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is the adjoint representation), then we recover the moduli space constructed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch02</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">GLSS08</a>]</cite> (resp. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">GS05</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Another important aspect of the theory of semistable vector bundles on a curve is the existence of a canonical filtration for any unstable vector bundle. This is called the Harder-Narasimhan filtration, and it is such that the associated graded object is a direct sum of semistable sheaves. This is helpful because unstable vector bundles are then constructed as extensions of semistable bundles, thus allowing us to do recursive arguments to study properties of the moduli space, as done by Harder-Narasimhan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">HN75</a>]</cite> and Atiyah-Bott <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AB83</a>]</cite>. More generally, for principal <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles on a curve Behrend <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beh95</a>]</cite> and Biswas-Holla <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH04</a>]</cite> constructed the Harder-Narasimhan reduction to a parabolic subgroup <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\subset G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\subset G</annotation></semantics></math>. An important property is that the associated principal <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle is semistable, where <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=G/P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=G/P</annotation></semantics></math> is the Levi factor of <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For torsion free sheaves over <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, we can construct two different Harder-Narasimhan filtrations, depending on the stability condition we use, slope or Gieseker stability.
They are respectively called slope-Harder-Narasimhan and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For principal <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves over <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> we only find in the literature the slope version of the Harder-Narasimhan filtration, constructed at the level of points by Anchouche-Azad-Biswas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AAB02</a>]</cite> and in families by Gurjar-Nitsure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">GN18</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">GN16</a>]</cite>. Biswas and Zamora <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">BZ16</a>]</cite> showed that the notion of Gieseker filtration is not well-behaved with respect to change of group. The strategy of considering the canonical filtration of the adjoint vector bundle does not yield in general a well-defined notion of Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan reduction in higher dimensions. Therefore a new approach needs to be introduced.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Our second main result (proven in section <a href="#S7" title="7. Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations for 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> (Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm18" title="Theorem 7.18. ‣ 7.5. The GHN filtration in families ‣ 7. Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations for 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.18</span></a>)) is the definition of a Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan parabolic reduction in the context of <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every principal <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf admits a uniquely defined multi-weighted filtration called the Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtration (abbreviated GHN filtration), see Definition <a href="#S7.Thmthm10" title="Definition 7.10. ‣ 7.3. The GHN filtration for 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 7. Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations for 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.10</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This filtration is schematic, in the sense that the universal stacks for relative GHN filtrations induce a locally closed stratification of <math id="Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Bun</mtext><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the group <math id="Thmtheorem2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is split, then the GHN filtration comes from a multi-weighted parabolic reduction defined over a big open subset (cf. Definition <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Definition 2.2. ‣ 2.2. Big open sets and torsion-free sheaves ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>). The associated Levi <math id="Thmtheorem2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\vec{\lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="1.140em" stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\vec{\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf is semistable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">If the representation <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is central, then our GHN filtration refines the canonical slope reduction constructed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AAB02</a>]</cite>. In this case, the resulting stratification of the stack <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Bun</mtext><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> refines the canonical slope stratification on the stack of <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles that was recently constructed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">GN18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Our main tools originate in some recent work on the theory of stacks. Alper, Halpern-Leistner and Heinloth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AHLH23</a>]</cite> have recently developed a theory which produces moduli spaces for Artin stacks, generalizing results of Keel-Mori on Deligne-Mumford stacks. This can be combined with the theory of <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-stability and <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-stratifications by Halpern-Leistner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>]</cite> to produce a powerful stack-theoretic approach to the construction of moduli spaces and canonical filtrations (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>, §5]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, §2.5]</cite> for more details). Halpern-Leistner defines in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>]</cite> a notion of filtration of an object in an Artin stack. Given certain cohomology classes on the stack, one can define a numerical invariant on filtrations. An object in the stack is called semistable if the numerical function is non-positive for all filtrations. If the numerical invariant satisfies certain properties, then the open substack of semistable objects admits a <em id="S1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">good moduli space</em> (as defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alp13</a>]</cite>). This is an intrinsic way of constructing the moduli space, in the sense that we do not need to choose a parameter space nor the action of a group.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Here are the main steps involved in this general approach:</p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(Step 1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define a (polynomial) numerical invariant <math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> on the stack (Subsection <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. The numerical invariant ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(Step 2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prove that the numerical invariant <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies some monotonicity properties (Subsection <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Monotonicity of the numerical invariant ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(Step 3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Show that <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the HN boundedness condition (Subsection <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. HN boundedness ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(Step 4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prove boundedness of the semistable locus (Subsection <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Boundedness and moduli space for the semistable locus ‣ 5. Moduli space for the semistable locus ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">In this article we carry out this program for the moduli stack
of principal <math id="S1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves, thus obtaining the intrinsic stack-theoretic construction of the moduli space described in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Furthermore, this theory provides a <math id="S1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-stratification of <math id="S1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Bun</mtext><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, which yields a canonical filtration (called the leading term HN filtration) for each unstable object. We apply this construction iteratively to the centers of the <math id="S1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-stratification to define the GHN reduction from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">In Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations in positive characteristic ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> we explain how to apply the same techniques to define the GHN filtration for <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves in arbitrary characteristic (see <a href="#A1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem A.4. ‣ Appendix A Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations in positive characteristic ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Stack-theoretic approach vs. GIT</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We end our introduction with a discussion comparing the stack theoretic approach in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AHLH23</a>]</cite> with the usual construction of moduli spaces using Geometric Invariant
Theory. We hope that this can provide some intuition for those readers that are more familiar with the GIT approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The construction of the moduli space of torsion free sheaves
(and principal <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves)
follows the usual application of GIT. We first construct a scheme <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing all objects together
with an extra data (in this case an isomorphism between a fixed vector space <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>
and the space of sections <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathcal{E}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathcal{E}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> for a chosen <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\gg 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\gg 0</annotation></semantics></math> and
the sheaf <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to
each object). The group <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL(W)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the scheme <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. We
choose an ample line bundle on <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> together with a linearization of the action of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL(W)</annotation></semantics></math>. Mumford’s GIT shows that there is an open subset <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{ss}\subset R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{ss}\subset R</annotation></semantics></math> which admits a <em id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">good quotient</em> [Ses72], and this is the moduli space of semistable torsion-free sheaves we are looking for.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To identify the subset <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{ss}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{ss}</annotation></semantics></math> of semistable points we use the
Hilbert-Mumford criterion. For each point <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in R</annotation></semantics></math> and for each
one-parameter subgroup <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:\mathbb{G}_{m}\to\SL(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:\mathbb{G}_{m}\to\SL(W)</annotation></semantics></math>
we consider the weight <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\lambda,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\lambda,p)</annotation></semantics></math> of the
action of the one-parameter subgroup <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> on the fiber of the limiting
point <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{t\to 0}\lambda(t)\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{t\to 0}\lambda(t)\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math>. The point <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is semistable if and only if <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\lambda,p)\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\lambda,p)\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all one-parameter subgroups <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. We remark that, in the usual applications of GIT to the construction of moduli spaces involving sheaves, the one-parameter subgroup <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> produces a filtration of the sheaf, and the Hilbert-Mumford criterion becomes equivalent to checking certain
numerical condition over all filtrations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>]</cite>,
the one-parameter subgroups appearing in the Hilbert-Mumford criterion
of GIT are replaced by <em id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">filtered objects</em>. For any algebraic stack <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, a filtration of a
point <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\Spec k\to\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\Spec k\to\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Theta:=[\mathbb{A}^{1}/\mathbb{G}_{m}]\to\mathcal{M}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Theta:=[\mathbb{A}^{1}/\mathbb{G}_{m}]\to\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and an identification
<math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(1)\simeq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(1)\simeq p</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a group homomorphism from <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>
to the automorphism group of the
object <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(0)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be thought of as the graded object
associated to the filtration <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For understanding these notions it is useful to let <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be the moduli
stack of torsion free sheaves on a projective scheme <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the Rees
construction (cf. Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 3.8. ‣ 3.2. Filtrations of 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>), a decreasing <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-indexed filtration</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\subset\cdots\subset\mathcal{E}_{i+1}\subset\mathcal{E}_{i}\subset\cdots\subset\mathcal{E}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\subset\cdots\subset\mathcal{E}_{i+1}\subset\mathcal{E}_{i}\subset\cdots\subset\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">of a torsion free sheaf <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> determines an <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-family <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> of torsion free sheaves on <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}|_{X_{t}}\cong\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}|_{X_{t}}\cong\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}|_{X_{0}}\cong\oplus\mathcal{E}_{i}/\mathcal{E}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">≅</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}|_{X_{0}}\cong\oplus\mathcal{E}_{i}/\mathcal{E}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The group <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> has a natural action on the
graded object <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}|_{X_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}|_{X_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with weight <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> on each
piece <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{i}/\mathcal{E}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{i}/\mathcal{E}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The family <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a morphism <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}\to\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}\to\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and the standard action
of <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence this morphism descends to
a map from the quotient stack <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbb{A}^{1}/\mathbb{G}_{m}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbb{A}^{1}/\mathbb{G}_{m}]</annotation></semantics></math>,
providing a filtration <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1.1. Stack-theoretic approach vs. GIT ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
One can define a function <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> which assigns a real number for each filtration <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as
in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1.1. Stack-theoretic approach vs. GIT ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). This is done in a similar way to GIT: we choose
a line bundle <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and define <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(f)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the weight
of the action of <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> on the fiber of <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L|_{f(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L|_{f(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In practice this approach has an important advantage: in the GIT case
we are looking at the action of <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL(W)</annotation></semantics></math>. This produces a
filtration on the vector space <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\cong H^{0}(\mathcal{E}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\cong H^{0}(\mathcal{E}(n))</annotation></semantics></math>, which in turn produces a
filtration on <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To show that the numerical condition of the Hilbert-Mumford criterion given by <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\lambda,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\lambda,p)</annotation></semantics></math> matches the stability condition on Hilbert polynomials is an involved calculation.
On the other hand, if we follow the intrinsic approach of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>]</cite>, we
are directly looking at a filtration on <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and the action of <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>
on the associated graded sheaf. This makes it easier to obtain the
stability condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of principal <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles the advantage of the new approach
is even more
clear. In the GIT setup we are looking at all filtrations of <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\cong H^{0}(\mathcal{E}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\cong H^{0}(\mathcal{E}(n))</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is not easy to show that it is enough to look
at filtrations coming from 1-parameter subgroups of <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, in the
theory of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hei17</a>]</cite> we only look at these subgroups from
the very beginning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Both GIT and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>]</cite> can be used to produce filtrations
of unstable objects. We recall how this works in GIT. Let <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in R</annotation></semantics></math> be an unstable point.
Among all the 1-parameter subgroups
<math id="S1.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL(W)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\lambda,p)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\lambda,p)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we can find one for which this weight is
maximal (after some normalization). This defines the Kempf filtration (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kem78</a>]</cite>) of <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. This filtration depends on the choices that were made in the
GIT construction (the number <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the parameter space <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and
the linearization). For torsion free sheaves it can
be shown that it is independent of the choice of <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\gg 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\gg 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and
it coincides with the Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan
filtration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">GSZ15</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zam13</a>]</cite>. Unfortunately, the computations become too involved in the case of principal <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles, so we do not obtain a Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtration following this approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zam16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In the theory in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>]</cite>, given an unstable object <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathcal{M}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathcal{M}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> we can also find a filtration <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(1)\simeq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(1)\simeq p</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(f)</annotation></semantics></math> is maximal. This program was carried out for <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-modules and pairs in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>]</cite>. It should be pointed out that the filtration
<math id="S1.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> which maximizes <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> does not have the property that the graded pieces of the filtration are semistable. But if we apply this procedure inductively we obtain the Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In this article we show that a similar story applies in the case of principal <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves. We use this to define what we
call the Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan reduction of an unstable
<math id="S1.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgments:</span> This project was started during the workshop “Moduli problems beyond geometric invariant theory” at the American Institute of Mathematics. We would like to thank the American Institute of Mathematics and the organizers that made the workshop possible. We would like to thank V. Balaji, Federico Fuentes, Daniel Halpern-Leistner and Jochen Heinloth for helpful remarks. We would also like to thank an anonymous referee for thoughtful comments on the manuscript.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">This work is supported
by grants CEX2019-000904-S and PID2019-108936GB-C21 (funded by MCIN/AEI/ 10.13039/501100011033), and NSF grants DMS-1454893 and DMS-2001071.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Notation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will work over a field <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of characteristic <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aff_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aff_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the category of affine schemes over <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. More generally, for any <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> we let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aff_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aff_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be the category of (absolutely) affine schemes equipped with a morphism to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sch_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sch</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sch_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sch_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sch</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sch_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the category of all schemes over <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Unless otherwise stated, all schemes will be understood to belong to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sch_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sch</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sch_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. An undecorated product of schemes (e.g. <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times S</annotation></semantics></math>) is understood to be a fiber product over <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. We might sometimes write <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All of the sheaves that we consider in this paper are quasicoherent. Whenever we write “<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-module” we mean a quasicoherent <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-module. In order to ease notation for pullbacks, we use the following convention. Whenever we have a map of schemes <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:Y\rightarrow T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:Y\rightarrow T</annotation></semantics></math> and a sheaf <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, we will write <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}|_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}|_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the pullback <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, if the morphism <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is clear from context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We fix a smooth projective geometrically connected variety <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> the dimension of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. We also choose once and for all an ample line bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. For any coherent sheaf <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> the Hilbert polynomial of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the line bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is a torsion-free sheaf on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then this is a polynomial of degree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in the variable <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> which can be written in the form <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathcal{F}}(n)=\sum_{i=0}^{d}\frac{a_{i}}{i!}n^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathcal{F}}(n)=\sum_{i=0}^{d}\frac{a_{i}}{i!}n^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
for some sequence of rational numbers <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL97</a>, Lemma 1.2.1]</cite>). We define the reduced Hilbert polynomial to be <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{p}_{\mathcal{F}}\vcentcolon=\frac{1}{a_{d}}P_{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{p}_{\mathcal{F}}\vcentcolon=\frac{1}{a_{d}}P_{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For every <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, we set <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mu}_{i}(\mathcal{F})\vcentcolon=\frac{a_{i}}{a_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mu}_{i}(\mathcal{F})\vcentcolon=\frac{a_{i}}{a_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We call this the <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> slope of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. The number <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mu}_{d-1}(\mathcal{F})=\frac{a_{d-1}}{a_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mu}_{d-1}(\mathcal{F})=\frac{a_{d-1}}{a_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> is simply called the slope of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We define an ordering for polynomials where, for any two <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},p_{2}\in\mathbb{R}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},p_{2}\in\mathbb{R}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}\geq p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}\geq p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}(n)\geq p_{2}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}(n)\geq p_{2}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\gg 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\gg 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Fix a reductive linear algebraic group <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k[G]</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the coordinate ring of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, which has the structure of a commutative Hopf algebra. We will denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> the moduli stack of principal <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles on <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. This is an algebraic stack that has affine diagonal and is locally of finite type over <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> (by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">HR19</a>, Thm. 1.2]</cite> taking <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=BG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=BG</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Fix a <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math> the group of linear automorphisms of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. We choose once and for all a faithful representation <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:G\rightarrow\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:G\rightarrow\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper we sometimes use the following notion which already appeared in the introduction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We say that a homomorphism of algebraic groups <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:G_{1}\to G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:G_{1}\to G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is central if the image of
the identity component of the center of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is in the center of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL(V)</annotation></semantics></math> the closed algebraic subgroup of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of linear automorphisms <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> such that the <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> exterior power <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{r}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{r}g</annotation></semantics></math> induces the identity on <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigwedge^{r}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mi>r</mi></msup><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigwedge^{r}V</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Set <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> to be the quotient stack <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbb{A}^{1}_{k}/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbb{A}^{1}_{k}/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Here by convention we always equip <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}_{k}=\Spec(k[t])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}_{k}=\Spec(k[t])</annotation></semantics></math> with the <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-action that gives the coordinate function <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> weight <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Big open sets and torsion-free sheaves</h3>

<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. An open subscheme <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be big (relative to <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>) if for all points <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> the complement <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}\setminus U_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}\setminus U_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> of the fiber <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed subset of codimension at least 2 in <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\hookrightarrow X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\hookrightarrow X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be a big open subset in <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the following are satisfied</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all points </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X_{S}\setminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X_{S}\setminus U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the local ring </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S},x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S},x}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has depth at least 2.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is scheme theoretically dense in </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:Y\rightarrow X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:Y\rightarrow X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be an affine morphism of finite type. Any section of </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> defined over </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> extends uniquely to a section defined over the whole </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X_{S}\setminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X_{S}\setminus U</annotation></semantics></math> be a point with image <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. By assumption the point <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension at least 2 in <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, this means that <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{s},x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{s},x}</annotation></semantics></math> has depth at least <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. So there exists a regular sequence <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{f}_{1},\overline{f}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{f}_{1},\overline{f}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{s},x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{s},x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Lift this sequence to a pair of elements <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},f_{2}\in\mathcal{O}_{X_{S},x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},f_{2}\in\mathcal{O}_{X_{S},x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S},x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S},x}</annotation></semantics></math> is flat over <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{S,s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{S,s}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can apply <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta23</a>, <a href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00ME" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Tag 00ME</a>]</cite> twice to conclude that <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a regular sequence. First we apply <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta23</a>, <a href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00ME" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Tag 00ME</a>]</cite> with <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=M=\mathcal{O}_{X_{S},x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=M=\mathcal{O}_{X_{S},x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we apply this result a second time with <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=M=\mathcal{O}_{X_{S},x}/(f_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=M=\mathcal{O}_{X_{S},x}/(f_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By definition, the scheme theoretic closure of <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is cut out by the ideal sheaf of <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> that locally consists of sections whose restriction to <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes. In other words, it is cut out by the kernel of the unit <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}\rightarrow j_{*}j^{*}\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}\rightarrow j_{*}j^{*}\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By part <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a)</annotation></semantics></math> every point outside <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> has depth at least <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore the unit map <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}\rightarrow j_{*}j^{*}\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}\rightarrow j_{*}j^{*}\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta23</a>, <a href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0E9I" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Tag 0E9I</a>]</cite>. We conclude that the scheme theoretic closure is cut out by the <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> ideal, i.e. it is the whole <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Uniqueness of the extension follows because <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is scheme theoretically dense in <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and the morphism <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\rightarrow X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\rightarrow X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is separated. It remains to show existence. Since extensions are unique, any set of local extensions will glue. Therefore we can work Zariski locally. Let <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be an affine open subset of <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type over <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, the scheme <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as a closed subscheme of <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{n}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{n}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\cap V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\cap V</annotation></semantics></math> is scheme theoretically dense inside <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, any extension of the section to <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{n}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{n}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> will automatically factor through the closed subscheme <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. It therefore suffices to extend the composition <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:U\cap V\rightarrow Y_{U\cap V}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{A}^{n}_{U\cap V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:U\cap V\rightarrow Y_{U\cap V}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{A}^{n}_{U\cap V}</annotation></semantics></math> to a map <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{p}:V\rightarrow\mathbb{A}^{n}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{p}:V\rightarrow\mathbb{A}^{n}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. This amounts to taking a section of <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{U\cap V}^{\oplus n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{U\cap V}^{\oplus n}</annotation></semantics></math> and extending to a section of <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{V}^{\oplus n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{V}^{\oplus n}</annotation></semantics></math>. This in turn follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta23</a>, <a href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0E9I" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Tag 0E9I</a>]</cite> applied to the sheaf <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{V}^{\oplus n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{V}^{\oplus n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the following definition, recall that the rank of a torsion-free sheaf on an integral scheme is the dimension of the generic fiber.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module. We say that <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is a relative torsion-free sheaf on <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> if it is <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-flat, finitely presented, and for all points <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> the fiber <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{X_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{X_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> is torsion-free. We say that <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> if all of the fibers <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{X_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{X_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> have rank <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From now on we will drop the adjective “relative” whenever it is clear from context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a torsion-free sheaf on <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, there exists a big open subset <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally-free.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> denote the (maximal) open subset of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally-free. Choose <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-flat and finitely presented, we can apply <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta23</a>, <a href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CZR" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Tag 0CZR</a>]</cite> to conclude that <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\cap X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\cap X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the set of points of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> where the fiber <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{X_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{X_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally-free. Since <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, all points of codimension <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> are discrete valuation rings. Therefore the torsion-free sheaf <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{X_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{X_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> is automatically free at all codimension <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> points. We conclude that the closed complement of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\cap X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\cap X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension at least <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Next we recall the notion of determinant for torsion-free sheaves, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kob87</a>, 5.6]</cite>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme and <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a finitely presented <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonnegative integer. There is a natural action of the symmetric group <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-letters on the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-fold tensor <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{\otimes h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{\otimes h}</annotation></semantics></math>, given by permuting the indexes. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sign}:S_{h}\rightarrow\{1,-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sign}:S_{h}\rightarrow\{1,-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the sign character.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be as in the paragraph above. We denote by <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{Alt,h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{Alt,h}</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-submodule of <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{\otimes h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{\otimes h}</annotation></semantics></math> that is locally generated over each affine open by sections of the form <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma\cdot(x_{1}\otimes x_{2}\otimes\cdots\otimes x_{h})-\text{sign}(\sigma)\,x_{1}\otimes x_{2}\otimes\cdots\otimes x_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">sign</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo rspace="0.130em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\cdot(x_{1}\otimes x_{2}\otimes\cdots\otimes x_{h})-\text{sign}(\sigma)\,x_{1}\otimes x_{2}\otimes\cdots\otimes x_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote by <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigwedge^{h}\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mi>h</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigwedge^{h}\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> the quotient sheaf <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{\otimes h}/\,\mathcal{G}_{\text{Alt, h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Alt, h</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{\otimes h}/\,\mathcal{G}_{\text{Alt, h}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigwedge^{h}\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mi>h</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigwedge^{h}\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finitely presented <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf by definition. The formation of <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigwedge^{h}\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mi>h</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigwedge^{h}\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with arbitrary base-change, because taking pullbacks is right exact and commutes with tensor products.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme and let <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a torsion-free sheaf of rank <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\mathcal{F})\vcentcolon=\left(\bigwedge^{h}\mathcal{F}\right)^{\vee\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em">⋀</mo><mi>h</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∨</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∨</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\mathcal{F})\vcentcolon=\left(\bigwedge^{h}\mathcal{F}\right)^{\vee\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For all morphisms of <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math> there is a canonical morphism <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\mathcal{F})|_{X_{T}}\rightarrow\det\left(\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\mathcal{F})|_{X_{T}}\rightarrow\det\left(\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. It turns out that this is an isomorphism, as shown in the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme and let <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a torsion-free sheaf on <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the rank of the fibers is constant. Then:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\hookrightarrow X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\hookrightarrow X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be a big open subscheme such that </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is locally-free. Then </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\mathcal{F})=j_{*}\left(\det(\mathcal{F}|_{U})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\mathcal{F})=j_{*}\left(\det(\mathcal{F}|_{U})\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\mathcal{F}|_{U})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\mathcal{F}|_{U})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on the right hand side denotes the usual determinant of a locally-free sheaf.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a line bundle on </span><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all morphisms of </span><math id="S2.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-schemes </span><math id="S2.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the canonical morphism </span><math id="S2.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\mathcal{F})|_{X_{T}}\rightarrow\det\left(\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\mathcal{F})|_{X_{T}}\rightarrow\det\left(\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By construction, <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\mathcal{F})|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\mathcal{F})|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the usual determinant of <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> as a locally-free sheaf. Therefore it suffices to show that the unit <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow j_{*}\left(\det(\mathcal{F})|_{U}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">det</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow j_{*}\left(\det(\mathcal{F})|_{U}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta23</a>, <a href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0E9I" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Tag 0E9I</a>]</cite> it suffices to check that the sheaf <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\mathcal{F})=\Hom\left(\left(\bigwedge^{h}\mathcal{F}\right)^{\vee},\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em">⋀</mo><mi>h</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\mathcal{F})=\Hom\left(\left(\bigwedge^{h}\mathcal{F}\right)^{\vee},\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> has depth at least <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> at all points in the complement of <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that this follows from <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2)</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta23</a>, <a href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AV5" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Tag 0AV5</a>]</cite>, since <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> has depth at least <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> at all points in the complement of <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2.2. Big open sets and torsion-free sheaves ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One can define a notion of determinant <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{d}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{d}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> using a finite resolution by locally-free sheaves, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL97</a>, pg. 36,37]</cite>. By definition, <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{d}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{d}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle that agrees with <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> on the open subset <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. By the same proof as in part <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a)</annotation></semantics></math> applied to the line bundle <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{d}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{d}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{d}(\mathcal{F})\cong j_{*}(\det(\mathcal{F}|_{U})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{d}(\mathcal{F})\cong j_{*}(\det(\mathcal{F}|_{U})</annotation></semantics></math>. We conclude that <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\mathcal{F})=j_{*}\left(\det(\mathcal{F}|_{U})\right)=\text{d}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\mathcal{F})=j_{*}\left(\det(\mathcal{F}|_{U})\right)=\text{d}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This follows because the notion of determinant <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{d}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{d}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL97</a>, pg. 36,37]</cite> using a fixed choice of a resolution can be seen to be compatible with base-change, by pulling back the resolution.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension of the vector space <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> that we use to define the faithful representation of the group <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The following is an open substack of the algebraic stack of coherent sheaves on <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">LMB00</a>, 4.6.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the pseudofunctor from <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\Aff_{k}\right)^{op}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\Aff_{k}\right)^{op}</annotation></semantics></math> into groupoids defined as follows. For any affine scheme <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\Aff_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\Aff_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we set</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)\,(T)\;=\;\left[\begin{matrix}\;\;\text{groupoid of $T$-flat relative torsion-free sheaves $\mathcal{F}$ of rank $r$ on $X_{T}$}\;\;\end{matrix}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.498em">=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">groupoid of </mtext><mi>T</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-flat relative torsion-free sheaves </mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> of rank </mtext><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> on </mtext><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)\,(T)\;=\;\left[\begin{matrix}\;\;\text{groupoid of $T$-flat relative torsion-free sheaves $\mathcal{F}$ of rank $r$ on $X_{T}$}\;\;\end{matrix}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an algebraic stack with affine diagonal and locally of finite type over <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the rank of a family of torsion-free sheaves is a locally constant function on the base, <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Coh^{tf}_{r}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Coh^{tf}_{r}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is an open substack of the stack <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Coh^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Coh^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> that parametrizes all torsion-free sheaves. The proposition follows, because <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Coh^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Coh^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic stack with affine diagonal and locally of finite type over <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, Prop. 2.5]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The stack <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> contains the stack <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{r}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{r}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundles on <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> as an open substack.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>The scheme of <math id="S2.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-reductions</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme and let <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)(S)</annotation></semantics></math> be a rank <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> torsion-free sheaf on <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this subsection we modify the construction in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch02</a>]</cite> in order to define a scheme of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-reductions of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. In our convention the representation <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual of the representation in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch02</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. Let <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a finitely presented <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-module. We can form the universal graded associative algebra <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\mathcal{G}):=\bigoplus_{j=0}^{\infty}\mathcal{G}^{\otimes j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\mathcal{G}):=\bigoplus_{j=0}^{\infty}\mathcal{G}^{\otimes j}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a graded ideal that on each affine open is generated by sections of the form <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\otimes y-y\otimes x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\otimes y-y\otimes x</annotation></semantics></math>. The quotient of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\mathcal{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\mathcal{G})</annotation></semantics></math> by this ideal is the symmetric algebra <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym^{\bullet}(\mathcal{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym^{\bullet}(\mathcal{G})</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym^{\bullet}(\mathcal{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym^{\bullet}(\mathcal{G})</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded commutative <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra that is locally finitely presented as an algebra.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that the sheaf <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k[G]</annotation></semantics></math>-comodule structure. This induces a comodule structure on <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\mathcal{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\mathcal{G})</annotation></semantics></math>, where we equip each graded component <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{\otimes j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{\otimes j}</annotation></semantics></math> with the tensor comodule structure. The ideal used to form <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym^{\bullet}(\mathcal{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym^{\bullet}(\mathcal{G})</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k[G]</annotation></semantics></math>-subcomodule. It follows that the quotient <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym^{\bullet}(\mathcal{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym^{\bullet}(\mathcal{G})</annotation></semantics></math> acquires a <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k[G]</annotation></semantics></math>-comodule structure that is compatible with the multiplication and the grading.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme and let <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a torsion-free sheaf of rank <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. We set</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{V}(\mathcal{F})\vcentcolon=\Sym^{\bullet}(\mathcal{F}\otimes_{k}V^{\vee})\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}}\Sym^{\bullet}(\det(\mathcal{F})^{\vee}\otimes_{k}\det(V))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub></msub><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false">det</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>k</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{V}(\mathcal{F})\vcentcolon=\Sym^{\bullet}(\mathcal{F}\otimes_{k}V^{\vee})\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}}\Sym^{\bullet}(\det(\mathcal{F})^{\vee}\otimes_{k}\det(V))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We also define <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mathcal{F},V)\vcentcolon=\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}(E_{V}(\mathcal{F}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathcal{F},V)\vcentcolon=\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}(E_{V}(\mathcal{F}))</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the structure morphism <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mathcal{F},V)\rightarrow X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathcal{F},V)\rightarrow X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is affine and of finite presentation.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The vector space <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> acquires a left <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k[G]</annotation></semantics></math>-comodule structure <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\rightarrow V\otimes k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\rightarrow V\otimes k[G]</annotation></semantics></math> via the representation <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:G\rightarrow\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:G\rightarrow\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. We equip <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> with the dual right comodule structure <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:V^{\vee}\rightarrow V^{\vee}\otimes k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:V^{\vee}\rightarrow V^{\vee}\otimes k[G]</annotation></semantics></math>. This induces a comodule structure <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\otimes_{k}V^{\vee}\rightarrow(\mathcal{F}\otimes_{k}V^{\vee})\otimes k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\otimes_{k}V^{\vee}\rightarrow(\mathcal{F}\otimes_{k}V^{\vee})\otimes k[G]</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{id}_{\mathcal{F}}\otimes c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>id</mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{id}_{\mathcal{F}}\otimes c</annotation></semantics></math>. By the discussion above, the graded algebra <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym^{\bullet}(\mathcal{F}\otimes_{k}V^{\vee})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym^{\bullet}(\mathcal{F}\otimes_{k}V^{\vee})</annotation></semantics></math> inherits a right <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k[G]</annotation></semantics></math>-comodule structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(V)</annotation></semantics></math> acquires a left <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k[G]</annotation></semantics></math>-comodule structure via the determinant representation of <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. By post-composing with the antipode of the Hopf algebra <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k[G]</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. pre-composing the representation with inversion on <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>), we get a right <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k[G]</annotation></semantics></math>-comodule structure on <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. In a similar way as above, this induces a right <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k[G]</annotation></semantics></math>-comodule structure on the algebra <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym^{\bullet}(\det(\mathcal{F})^{\vee}\otimes_{k}\det(V))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">det</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0.055em">det</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>k</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym^{\bullet}(\det(\mathcal{F})^{\vee}\otimes_{k}\det(V))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">By taking the tensor comodule structure for the <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k[G]</annotation></semantics></math>-comodules defined above, we can equip <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{V}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{V}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> with a right <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k[G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k[G]</annotation></semantics></math>-comodule structure</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{E_{V}(\mathcal{F})}:E_{V}(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow E_{V}(\mathcal{F})\otimes_{k}k[G]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{E_{V}(\mathcal{F})}:E_{V}(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow E_{V}(\mathcal{F})\otimes_{k}k[G]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Since this comodule structure is compatible with the algebra multiplication, it translates into a left action of the algebraic group <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on the scheme <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mathcal{F},V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathcal{F},V)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the subalgebra <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{V}(\mathcal{F})^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{V}(\mathcal{F})^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariants of <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{V}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{V}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that this is defined over each affine open as the <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>-subalgebra of <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{V}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{V}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of sections <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{E_{V}(\mathcal{F})}(s)=s\otimes 1_{k[G]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{E_{V}(\mathcal{F})}(s)=s\otimes 1_{k[G]}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the characteristic of <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is reductive, the algebra of invariants <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{V}(\mathcal{F})^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{V}(\mathcal{F})^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a locally finitely presented <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra and its formation is compatible with arbitrary base-change. This can be checked affine locally, and over each affine it follows from the existence of the Reynolds operator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">MFK94</a>, Chpt. 1, §1,2]</cite>. By definition, the scheme <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left((E_{V}(\mathcal{F}))^{G}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left((E_{V}(\mathcal{F}))^{G}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the affine GIT quotient <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(\mathcal{F},V)/\!\!/\,G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathcal{F},V)/\!\!/\,G</annotation></semantics></math> relative to <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a big open subscheme of <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally-free. The restriction <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mathcal{F},V)|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathcal{F},V)|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is, by definition, the scheme</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\vcentcolon=\Hom\left(\mathcal{F}|_{U},V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U}\right)\times\Hom\left(\det(\mathcal{F}|_{U})^{\vee},\,\det(V^{\vee})\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.019em">,</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\vcentcolon=\Hom\left(\mathcal{F}|_{U},V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U}\right)\times\Hom\left(\det(\mathcal{F}|_{U})^{\vee},\,\det(V^{\vee})\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">parametrizing pairs <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{1},f_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{1},f_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> of homomorphisms of vector bundles</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}:\mathcal{F}|_{U}\rightarrow V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U}\;\;\;\;\text{and}\;\;\;\;f_{2}:\det(\mathcal{F}|_{U})^{\vee}\rightarrow\det(V^{\vee})\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0.880em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.111em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}:\mathcal{F}|_{U}\rightarrow V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U}\;\;\;\;\text{and}\;\;\;\;f_{2}:\det(\mathcal{F}|_{U})^{\vee}\rightarrow\det(V^{\vee})\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.3" class="ltx_p">This means that there is a universal pair <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{1},u_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{1},u_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> of morphisms</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}:\mathcal{F}|_{H}\rightarrow V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{H}\;\;\;\;\text{and}\;\;\;\;u_{2}:\det(\mathcal{F}|_{H})^{\vee}\rightarrow\det(V^{\vee})\otimes\mathcal{O}_{H}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0.880em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.111em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}:\mathcal{F}|_{H}\rightarrow V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{H}\;\;\;\;\text{and}\;\;\;\;u_{2}:\det(\mathcal{F}|_{H})^{\vee}\rightarrow\det(V^{\vee})\otimes\mathcal{O}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.4" class="ltx_p">We take the <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> wedge of <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to get a universal determinant morphism.</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{r}u_{1}:\det(\mathcal{F}|_{H})\rightarrow\det(V)\otimes\mathcal{O}_{H}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∧</mo><mi>r</mi></msup><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo lspace="0.111em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{r}u_{1}:\det(\mathcal{F}|_{H})\rightarrow\det(V)\otimes\mathcal{O}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.5" class="ltx_p">We set <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta_{U}\vcentcolon=(\wedge^{r}u_{1})\otimes u_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mi>r</mi></msup><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{U}\vcentcolon=(\wedge^{r}u_{1})\otimes u_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the canonical trivializations <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(V)\otimes\det(V^{\vee})\xrightarrow{\sim}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(V)\otimes\det(V^{\vee})\xrightarrow{\sim}k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\mathcal{F}|_{H})\otimes\det(\mathcal{F}|_{H})^{\vee}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{O}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0em">det</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\mathcal{F}|_{H})\otimes\det(\mathcal{F}|_{H})^{\vee}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{O}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can view this as a map <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{U}:\mathcal{O}_{H}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{U}:\mathcal{O}_{H}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can then form the map <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{U}-1:\mathcal{O}_{H}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{U}-1:\mathcal{O}_{H}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by subtracting the identity of <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{U}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{U}-1</annotation></semantics></math> cuts out the divisor on <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> given by the locus where <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism and <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the determinant of the inverse dual of <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular the vanishing locus of <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{U}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{U}-1</annotation></semantics></math> is canonically isomorphic to the scheme parametrizing linear isomorphisms of vector bundles <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Iso(\mathcal{F}|_{U},V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Iso</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Iso(\mathcal{F}|_{U},V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U})</annotation></semantics></math>. This identification is given by forgetting <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">By construction, the composition <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{H/\!\!/\,G}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{H}\xrightarrow{\delta_{U}-1}\mathcal{O}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.260em;" width="0.260em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">U</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{H/\!\!/\,G}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{H}\xrightarrow{\delta_{U}-1}\mathcal{O}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> lands in the <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-subalgebra of invariants <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E_{V}(\mathcal{F})|_{U})^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E_{V}(\mathcal{F})|_{U})^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore this composition induces a well-defined map <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{U}:\mathcal{O}_{H/\!\!/\,G}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{H/\!\!/\,G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.260em;" width="0.260em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.260em;" width="0.260em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{U}:\mathcal{O}_{H/\!\!/\,G}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}_{H/\!\!/\,G}</annotation></semantics></math> that cuts out an effective divisor on <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H/\!\!/\,G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H/\!\!/\,G</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote this closed subscheme by <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H/\!\!/\,G)_{\alpha_{U}=0}\hookrightarrow H/\!\!/\,G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H/\!\!/\,G)_{\alpha_{U}=0}\hookrightarrow H/\!\!/\,G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.13</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is defined to be the scheme theoretic closure of <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H/\!\!/\,G)_{\alpha_{U}=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H/\!\!/\,G)_{\alpha_{U}=0}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(\mathcal{F},V)/\!\!/\,G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathcal{F},V)/\!\!/\,G</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> is the scheme theoretic image of the composition</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H/\!\!/\,G)_{\alpha_{U}=0}\hookrightarrow H/\!\!/\,G\hookrightarrow H(\mathcal{F},V)/\!\!/\,G" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H/\!\!/\,G)_{\alpha_{U}=0}\hookrightarrow H/\!\!/\,G\hookrightarrow H(\mathcal{F},V)/\!\!/\,G</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the second map <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H/\!\!/\,G\hookrightarrow H(\mathcal{F},V)/\!\!/\,G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H/\!\!/\,G\hookrightarrow H(\mathcal{F},V)/\!\!/\,G</annotation></semantics></math> is the open immersion that comes from identifying
<math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H/\!\!/\,G\xrightarrow{\sim}\left(H(\mathcal{F},V)/\!\!/\,G\right)|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.160em;" width="0.160em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H/\!\!/\,G\xrightarrow{\sim}\left(H(\mathcal{F},V)/\!\!/\,G\right)|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>
via the compatibility of taking <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariants with flat base-change.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.14</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It should be noted that the construction of <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on the choice of <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, for any two big open subsets <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, the intersection <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}\cap U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}\cap U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is also big. Since the element <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is canonically defined above, the restrictions of <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{U_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{U_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{U_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{U_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the subset <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}\cap U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}\cap U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> agree with <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{U_{1}\cap U_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{U_{1}\cap U_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is always flat over <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, because it is locally given by a polynomial algebra. It follows that <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H/\!/\!\,G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.182em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H/\!/\!\,G</annotation></semantics></math> is also flat over <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, because taking <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> invariants is exact by the existence of the Reynolds operator. Since <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is scheme theoretically dense in <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2.2. Big open sets and torsion-free sheaves ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, it follows that the open <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H/\!/\!\,G)|_{U_{1}\cap U_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.182em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H/\!/\!\,G)|_{U_{1}\cap U_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is scheme theoretically dense in <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H/\!/\!\,G)|_{U_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.182em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H/\!/\!\,G)|_{U_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H/\!/\!\,G)|_{U_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.182em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H/\!/\!\,G)|_{U_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular the closures of <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m22" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H/\!/\!\,G)_{\alpha_{U_{1}}=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.182em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H/\!/\!\,G)_{\alpha_{U_{1}}=0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m23" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H/\!/\!\,G)_{\alpha_{U_{2}}=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.182em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H/\!/\!\,G)_{\alpha_{U_{2}}=0}</annotation></semantics></math> both agree with the closure of <math id="S2.Thmthm14.p1.m24" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H/\!/\!\,G)_{\alpha_{U_{1}\cap U_{2}}=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.182em">/</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H/\!/\!\,G)_{\alpha_{U_{1}\cap U_{2}}=0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma is an immediate consequence of the constructions above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.15</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The scheme <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> is affine and of finite presentation over <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. The formation of <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with base-change. In other words, for any morphism of <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math> there is a canonical isomorphism <math id="S2.Thmthm15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})\times_{X_{S}}X_{T}\xrightarrow{\sim}\Red_{G}\left(\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})\times_{X_{S}}X_{T}\xrightarrow{\sim}\Red_{G}\left(\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a big open subset of <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally-free. Since <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle of rank <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, we can naturally associate to it a <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.16</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be as above. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the corresponding <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle on <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a canonical identification of <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})|_{U}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{P}/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})|_{U}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{P}/G</annotation></semantics></math>. Here the quotient on the right is formed via the inclusion <math id="S2.Thmthm16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:G\hookrightarrow\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:G\hookrightarrow\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\delta_{U}=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\delta_{U}=1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the closed subscheme of <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> cut out by the equation <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{U}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{U}-1</annotation></semantics></math>, as described in the construction of <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> above. By definition, <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is the affine GIT quotient <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H_{\delta_{U}=1})/\!/\!\,G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.182em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{\delta_{U}=1})/\!/\!\,G</annotation></semantics></math>. We have seen that <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\delta_{U}=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\delta_{U}=1}</annotation></semantics></math> is canonically isomorphic to the <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Iso(\mathcal{F}|_{U},V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Iso</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Iso(\mathcal{F}|_{U},V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U})</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing isomorphisms of vector bundles. This identification respects the natural left <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-actions defined on both schemes via the inclusions <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:G\rightarrow\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:G\rightarrow\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">The map that takes an isomorphism <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:\mathcal{F}|_{U}\oplus\det(\mathcal{F})^{\vee}|_{U}\xrightarrow{\sim}(V\oplus\det(V^{\vee}))\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">det</mo><msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:\mathcal{F}|_{U}\oplus\det(\mathcal{F})^{\vee}|_{U}\xrightarrow{\sim}(V\oplus\det(V^{\vee}))\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> to its inverse in <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom\left((V\oplus\det(V^{\vee}))\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U},\,\mathcal{F}|_{U}\oplus\det(\mathcal{F})^{\vee}|_{U}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">det</mo><msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom\left((V\oplus\det(V^{\vee}))\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U},\,\mathcal{F}|_{U}\oplus\det(\mathcal{F})^{\vee}|_{U}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isomorphism of schemes</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-)^{-1}:\;\Iso(\mathcal{F}|_{U},V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U})\xrightarrow{\sim}\Iso\left(V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U},\,\mathcal{F}|_{U}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Iso</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Iso</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.297em">,</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-)^{-1}:\;\Iso(\mathcal{F}|_{U},V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U})\xrightarrow{\sim}\Iso\left(V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U},\,\mathcal{F}|_{U}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">The right-hand side admits a right action of <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math> given by precomposition. By definition <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Iso(V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U},\,\mathcal{F}|_{U})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Iso</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.297em">,</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Iso(V\otimes_{k}\mathcal{O}_{U},\,\mathcal{F}|_{U})</annotation></semantics></math> is the total space of the <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with its right <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-action. The inclusion <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:G\hookrightarrow\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:G\hookrightarrow\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math> induces a right <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. The inversion map <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> above intertwines the left <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-action on the left hand side and the right <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-action on the right hand side.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is faithful, it follows that <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts properly and freely on <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore the GIT quotient <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{P}/\!/\!\,G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo>/</mo></mpadded><mo rspace="0.182em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}/\!/\!\,G</annotation></semantics></math> and the quotient as étale sheaves <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}/G</annotation></semantics></math> coincide. This way we get the sought after canonical identification of <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})|_{U}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{P}/G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})|_{U}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{P}/G</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition explains the connection between the scheme <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-reductions of structure group.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.17</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any big open subset <math id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally-free, there is a bijection between the following two sets <math id="S2.Thmthm17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A),(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A),(B)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(A)</span> 
<div id="S2.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The set of sections of the structure morphism </span><math id="S2.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I5.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(B)</span> 
<div id="S2.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The set of </span><math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-reductions of structure group for the </span><math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I5.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-bundle </span><math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I5.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> corresponding to </span><math id="S2.I5.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I5.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text"> is an affine morphism of finite type. Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2.2. Big open sets and torsion-free sheaves ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> shows that the restriction of maps induces a bijection between sections <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:X_{S}\rightarrow\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:X_{S}\rightarrow\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> defined over the whole <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and sections <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{U}:U\rightarrow\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{U}:U\rightarrow\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> defined over <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. By Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm16" title="Proposition 2.16. ‣ 2.3. The scheme of 
          
            G
          
        -reductions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.16</span></a>, sections of <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})|_{U}\rightarrow U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})|_{U}\rightarrow U</annotation></semantics></math> are in canonical correspondence with sections of <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}/G\rightarrow U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}/G\rightarrow U</annotation></semantics></math> via the inversion map. A section of <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}/G\rightarrow U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}/G\rightarrow U</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as a <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-reduction of structure group for <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. This establishes the canonical bijection between <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Principal <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we define the stack of <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves and give a description of its filtrations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Principal <math id="S3.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves and the moduli stack <math id="S3.SS1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Bun</mtext><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Bun}_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. A principal <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf on <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a pair <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, where</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a family of torsion-free sheaves of rank </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a section of the structure morphism </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We will often omit the word “principal” and refer to these objects simply as <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm17" title="Proposition 2.17. ‣ 2.3. The scheme of 
          
            G
          
        -reductions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.17</span></a>, one can think of a <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf over <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> as the data of</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A torsion-free sheaf <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A reduction of structure group to <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math> bundle <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is (any) big open subscheme of <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally-free.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that the image of the representation <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the subgroup <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SL(V)\subset\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">SL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SL(V)\subset\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then our <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves can be viewed as singular principal <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">GLSS08</a>]</cite> for the dual representation <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{1},\sigma_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{1},\sigma_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{2},\sigma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{2},\sigma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> be two <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves on <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, an isomorphism <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:\mathcal{F}_{1}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{F}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:\mathcal{F}_{1}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{F}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isomorphism of <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-reduction schemes <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F}_{1})\xrightarrow{\sim}\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F}_{1})\xrightarrow{\sim}\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism of the <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves if the sections <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are identified under this induced isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using this notion of isomorphism, we endow the class of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves on <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with the structure of a groupoid. By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm15" title="Lemma 2.15. ‣ 2.3. The scheme of 
          
            G
          
        -reductions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.15</span></a>, the formation of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with base-change. In particular, it makes sense to pull back principal <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves under a morphism of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the pseudofunctor from <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\Aff_{k}\right)^{op}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\Aff_{k}\right)^{op}</annotation></semantics></math> into groupoids defined as follows. For any affine scheme <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\Aff_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\Aff_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we set</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)\,(T)\;=\;\left[\;\;\text{groupoid of $\rho$-sheaves $(\mathcal{F},\sigma)$ on $X_{T}$}\;\;\;\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.498em">=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">groupoid of </mtext><mi>ρ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-sheaves </mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> on </mtext><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)\,(T)\;=\;\left[\;\;\text{groupoid of $\rho$-sheaves $(\mathcal{F},\sigma)$ on $X_{T}$}\;\;\;\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There is a natural forgetful morphism <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)\mapsto\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)\mapsto\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The forgetful morphism <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is schematic, affine and of finite type. In particular <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic stack with affine diagonal and locally of finite type over <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\Aff_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\Aff_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose a morphism <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> represented by a relative torsion-free sheaf <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The fiber product <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)\times_{\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)\times_{\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)}T</annotation></semantics></math> is the functor that parametrizes sections of the structure morphism <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we know that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})\rightarrow X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is affine and of finite presentation, we can apply <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beh91</a>, Prop. 4.4.1]</cite> (or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">HR15</a>, Lemma 2.3 (i)]</cite> + <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">HR19</a>, Thm. 1.3 (ii)]</cite>) to conclude that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)\times_{\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)\times_{\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)}T</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a scheme that is relatively affine and of finite type over <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that we have an open immersion <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{r}(X)\hookrightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{r}(X)\hookrightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> from the stack <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{r}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{r}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundles on <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a Cartesian square</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.Ex12.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="75.26" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-37.63px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 207.06 75.26" width="207.06"><g transform="translate(0,75.26) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(103.53,0) translate(0,37.63)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -103.53 -29.11)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 75.26)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 15.44)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(43.23,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -37.27 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.34em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="60.04"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bun_{G}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bun_{G}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 88 0) translate(75.74,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -35.84 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.13em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="57.16"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bun_{\rho}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bun_{\rho}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 66.74)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 1.59 0) translate(41.64,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -35.68 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.11em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="56.86"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bun_{r}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Bun_{r}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 86.46 0) translate(77.27,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -37.37 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.35em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.21" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="60.22"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.39998pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.17 25.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="0" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)" width="7.69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xhookrightarrow{\;\;}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathvariant="italic">↪</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xhookrightarrow{\;\;}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -60.3 13.39 L -60.3 -12.83"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -60.3 -13.11)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -57.04 -1.14)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.83em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="11.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\rho_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">ρ</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\rho_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 60.2 12.89 L 60.2 -11.46"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 60.2 -11.74)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.39998pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -4.73 -25.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="0" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)" width="7.69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xhookrightarrow{\;\;}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathvariant="italic">↪</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xhookrightarrow{\;\;}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{G}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{G}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is the stack of principal <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles on <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the map on stacks induced by extension of the structure group via the representation <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\Aff_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\Aff_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The groupoid <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{r}(X)\,(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{r}(X)\,(S)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of points <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)\,(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)\,(S)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally-free on the entirety of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm17" title="Proposition 2.17. ‣ 2.3. The scheme of 
          
            G
          
        -reductions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.17</span></a> with <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that the fiber product <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{r}(X)\times_{\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)}\Bun_{\rho}(X)\,(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{r}(X)\times_{\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)}\Bun_{\rho}(X)\,(S)</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizes pairs <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{P},\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{P},\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle on <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is a reduction of structure group to <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. This groupoid is naturally isomorphic to the groupoid of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles on <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Filtrations of <math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explain the notion of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hei17</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AHLH23</a>]</cite> in the context of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves. For this subsection we will work over a field extension <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\supset k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊃</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\supset k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us recall the definition of a <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration. We denote by <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> the base-change <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[\,\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}/\mathbb{G}_{m}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.130em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[\,\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}/\mathbb{G}_{m}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>. The stack <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> has a unique closed point <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the origin of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. It also has an open point <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the image of the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-point <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be a stack over <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathcal{M}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathcal{M}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-point. A <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration of the point <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism of stacks <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> along with an isomorphism <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(1)\xrightarrow{\sim}p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(1)\xrightarrow{\sim}p</annotation></semantics></math>. The graded point <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f|_{[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f|_{[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}]}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the associated graded of the filtration. We say that the filtration is nondegenerate if the action of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> on the object <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(0)</annotation></semantics></math> is nontrivial.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We start by giving a concrete description of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtrations in the stack <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-point of <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Coh^{tf}_{r}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Coh^{tf}_{r}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. A <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{K}}</annotation></semantics></math>-subsheaves of <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the following properties</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m+1}\subset\mathcal{F}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m+1}\subset\mathcal{F}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m}=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\gg 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\gg 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m}=\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m}=\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\ll 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≪</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\ll 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is torsion-free.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An isomorphism between two such filtrations is an automorphism of <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> that identifies the filtrations. The graded object at the origin <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f|_{[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}]}:[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}]\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f|_{[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}]}:[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}]\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is the associated graded sheaf <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2)</annotation></semantics></math> above, we can associate a <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant coherent sheaf on <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> via the Rees construction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>, Prop. 1.0.1]</cite>. It follows from the construction that the corresponding morphism <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{K}\to\Coh(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{K}\to\Coh(X)</annotation></semantics></math> lands in the substack of torsion-free sheaves <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the condition <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3)</annotation></semantics></math> above is satisfied.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For any filtration <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> we can post-compose with the forgetful morphism <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> to get a filtration <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. This defines a functor <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> between the groupoid of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtrations of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-points of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and the groupoid of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtrations of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-points of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Coh_{r}^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The functor <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> defined above is faithful.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AHLH23</a>, Lem. 3.7 (3)]</cite>, which applies here because the forgetful morphism is affine by Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 3.6. ‣ 3.1. Principal 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves and the moduli stack 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                Bun
                ρ
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  X
                  ) ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This tells us that, if <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf, a filtration of <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is the same thing as a <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> (as in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 3.8. ‣ 3.2. Filtrations of 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>) that we call the underlying associated filtration, which satisfies some “compatibility conditions” that will be explained in the remainder of this section. First, we need to explain what is a filtration of <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles on any scheme <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a scheme over <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle on <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the following two groupoids are equivalent.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The groupoid of </span><math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-bundles </span><math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on </span><math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> along with an isomorphism of the restriction </span><math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}|_{U\times_{K}1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}|_{U\times_{K}1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The groupoid of decreasing </span><math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-weighted filtrations </span><math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> by vector subbundles </span><math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. An isomorphism between two filtrations is just an automorphism of </span><math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> that identifies the filtrations.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The equivalence is given by the Rees construction from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>, Prop. 1.0.1]</cite>, as in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 3.8. ‣ 3.2. Filtrations of 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>, we omit the details.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Now we are ready to give a first description of <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtrations for a <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf on <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. A filtration of <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> will consist of a pair <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\,\mathcal{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.297em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\,\mathcal{P})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a filtration of <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, as described in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 3.8. ‣ 3.2. Filtrations of 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\hookrightarrow X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\hookrightarrow X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be the maximal big open subset of <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> where the associated graded <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally-free. The second element of the pair <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle on <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the associated <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{*}(\mathcal{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{*}(\mathcal{P})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the restricted filtration <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m}|_{U})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m}|_{U})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> under the equivalence described in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Proposition 3.10. ‣ 3.2. Filtrations of 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The filtration <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m}|_{U})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m}|_{U})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> completely determines the underlying associated filtration <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is because each <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is saturated on <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, so it is determined in codimension 1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lan75</a>, pg. 90-92]</cite>. We can describe <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> more explicitly in terms of reflexive hulls. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the pushforward of the vector bundle <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> under the open immersion <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\hookrightarrow X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\hookrightarrow X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have that <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subsheaf of the reflexive hull <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{\vee\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo>∨</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∨</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{\vee\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we can recover <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> as the intersection <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}_{m}\cap\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}_{m}\cap\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> taken inside <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{\vee\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo>∨</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∨</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{\vee\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular the filtration is completely determined by the <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will, however, keep the underlying associated filtration as part of the notation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">A morphism between two such filtrations <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\mathcal{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\mathcal{P})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\mathcal{P}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\mathcal{P}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> consists of an isomorphism of <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:\mathcal{P}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:\mathcal{P}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> defined over some big open subset <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> where both <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined. We require that the associated isomorphism of <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles on <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{*}(\varphi):(\mathcal{F}_{m}|_{U})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\xrightarrow{\sim}(\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m}|_{U})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{*}(\varphi):(\mathcal{F}_{m}|_{U})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\xrightarrow{\sim}(\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m}|_{U})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a (unique) extension to an isomorphism of filtrations <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\varphi}:(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\xrightarrow{\sim}(\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\varphi}:(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\xrightarrow{\sim}(\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined over all of <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the description of the filtrations as intersections of reflexive hulls, it follows that we only need to check that the isomorphism <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{*}(\varphi)|_{U\times_{K}1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{*}(\varphi)|_{U\times_{K}1}</annotation></semantics></math> extends to an automorphism <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is automatically satisfied if <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is reflexive, but not otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf on <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will denote by <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Filt</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> the groupoid of pairs <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\mathcal{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\mathcal{P})</annotation></semantics></math>, with isomorphisms as described above.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf on <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a natural equivalence between the following two groupoids.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The groupoid of </span><math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-filtrations </span><math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of the </span><math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-sheaf </span><math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The groupoid </span><math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Filt</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">The groupoid of <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtrations classifies <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{\mathcal{F}},\widetilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{\mathcal{F}},\widetilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> along with an isomorphism of the restriction <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{\mathcal{F}},\widetilde{\sigma})|_{X\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{\mathcal{F}},\widetilde{\sigma})|_{X\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> with the original <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration of <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant section <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\sigma}:X\times\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}\rightarrow\Red_{G}(\widetilde{\mathcal{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\sigma}:X\times\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}\rightarrow\Red_{G}(\widetilde{\mathcal{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> extending <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:X\times 1\rightarrow\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:X\times 1\rightarrow\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>. By Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 3.8. ‣ 3.2. Filtrations of 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>, the bundle <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by applying the Rees construction to some <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be the maximal open in <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally-free. Then <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is a big open in <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}|_{U\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}|_{U\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally-free. By Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm17" title="Proposition 2.17. ‣ 2.3. The scheme of 
          
            G
          
        -reductions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.17</span></a>, the <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant section <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the data of a <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-reduction of the <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}|_{U\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}|_{U\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is by definition a <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the associated <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{*}(\mathcal{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{*}(\mathcal{P})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> recovers the restriction of the filtration <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m}|_{U})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m}|_{U})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> under the equivalence in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Proposition 3.10. ‣ 3.2. Filtrations of 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>. This shows how to define the functor on objects, and it also shows that it is essentially surjective. The definition of the functor on morphisms and the fact that it is fully faithful follows from the definition of the isomorphisms in the groupoid <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Filt</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Filtrations of <math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves in the split case</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will describe the elements of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Filt</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> more explicitly in the case when <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is split. For that purpose, we need a better description of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles on <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is done in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hei17</a>, 1.F]</cite>, which gives a group theoretic description in terms of parabolic reductions and a generalized Rees construction<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            We note that some of the discussion in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hei17</a>, 1.F]</cite> applies without the hypothesis that <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is split. However, the results are more concrete under this assumption.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We start by recalling some facts about parabolic subgroups of reductive groups. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a reductive group over <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> be a cocharacter <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:\mathbb{G}_{m}\rightarrow H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:\mathbb{G}_{m}\rightarrow H</annotation></semantics></math>. We can associate a corresponding parabolic subgroup <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, its <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-points are</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}(T)=\left\{\,p\in H(T)\,\mid\,\lim\limits_{x\to 0}\,\lambda(x)\,p\,\lambda(x)^{-1}\;\text{exists}\,\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.130em">{</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.130em" rspace="0.019em">∣</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>exists</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}(T)=\left\{\,p\in H(T)\,\mid\,\lim\limits_{x\to 0}\,\lambda(x)\,p\,\lambda(x)^{-1}\;\text{exists}\,\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, the cocharater <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> induces a Levi decomposition <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}=L_{\lambda}\ltimes U_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}=L_{\lambda}\ltimes U_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\lambda}(T)=\left\{\,u\in H(T)\,\mid\,\lim\limits_{x\to 0}\,\lambda(x)\,u\,\lambda(x)^{-1}=1_{H}\,\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.130em">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.130em" rspace="0.019em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\lambda}(T)=\left\{\,u\in H(T)\,\mid\,\lim\limits_{x\to 0}\,\lambda(x)\,u\,\lambda(x)^{-1}=1_{H}\,\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\lambda}(T)=\left\{\,l\in H(T)\,\mid\,\lambda(x)\,l\,\lambda(x)^{-1}=l\,\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.130em">{</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.130em" rspace="0.130em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\lambda}(T)=\left\{\,l\in H(T)\,\mid\,\lambda(x)\,l\,\lambda(x)^{-1}=l\,\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.13</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a reductive linear algebraic group over <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme and let <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle on <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. A weighted parabolic reduction is a pair <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,\mathcal{H}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,\mathcal{H}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:\mathbb{G}_{m}\rightarrow H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:\mathbb{G}_{m}\rightarrow H</annotation></semantics></math> is a cocharacter and <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a reduction of structure group of <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> to the parabolic subgroup <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta:H\rightarrow F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta:H\rightarrow F</annotation></semantics></math> be a homomorphism of reductive groups over <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. For every cocharacter <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:\mathbb{G}_{m}\rightarrow H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:\mathbb{G}_{m}\rightarrow H</annotation></semantics></math>, we can compose with <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a cocharacter <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\circ\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\circ\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to a well defined homomorphism of parabolic subgroups <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{P}:P_{\lambda}\rightarrow P_{\theta\circ\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{P}:P_{\lambda}\rightarrow P_{\theta\circ\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that we have an <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on a <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we can define an <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> via extension of structure group. If we have a parabolic reduction <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> to the subgroup <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, then by extension of structure group using <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> we get a parabolic reduction of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> to the subgroup <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\theta\circ\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\theta\circ\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. This way we can define a map <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> between weighted parabolic reductions of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and weighted parabolic reductions of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Having set up the notation for weighted parabolic reductions, we proceed to explain how this notion relates to <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hei17</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. Suppose that <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. We can equivalently view this as a <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> be a weighted parabolic reduction of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. The morphism <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:\mathbb{G}_{m}\rightarrow G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:\mathbb{G}_{m}\rightarrow G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> can be used to define a homomorphism <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{conj}_{\lambda}:P_{\lambda}\times\mathbb{G}_{m}\rightarrow P_{\lambda}\times\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>conj</mtext><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{conj}_{\lambda}:P_{\lambda}\times\mathbb{G}_{m}\rightarrow P_{\lambda}\times\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of group schemes over <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,x)\mapsto(\lambda(x)p\lambda(x)^{-1},\,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.297em">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,x)\mapsto(\lambda(x)p\lambda(x)^{-1},\,x)</annotation></semantics></math>. This extends to a homomorphism <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{gr}_{\lambda}:P_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}\rightarrow P_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>gr</mtext><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{gr}_{\lambda}:P_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}\rightarrow P_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed point.
The restriction of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{gr}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>gr</mtext><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{gr}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to a slice
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}\times\{t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}\times\{t\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity when <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>, it is an isomorphism when <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
and it is the projection of the parabolic
subgroup <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to its Levi <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now we can define a <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. Note that
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>-torsor on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the homomorphism <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{gr}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>gr</mtext><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{gr}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain, by extension of structure group, a new <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>-torsor which is denoted <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K})\times^{\text{gr}_{\lambda}}_{\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}}(P_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><msub><mtext>gr</mtext><mi>λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K})\times^{\text{gr}_{\lambda}}_{\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}}(P_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>. This bundle is <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant because the homomorphism <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{gr}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>gr</mtext><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{gr}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant (with the adjoint action on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> and multiplication on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>), and therefore we can take the quotient by <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\lambda)\vcentcolon=\left[\,\left((\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K})\times^{\text{gr}_{\lambda}}_{\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}}(P_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K})\right)\,/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}\,\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Rees</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.130em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><msub><mtext>gr</mtext><mi>λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.130em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\lambda)\vcentcolon=\left[\,\left((\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K})\times^{\text{gr}_{\lambda}}_{\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K}}(P_{\lambda}\times_{K}\mathbb{A}^{1}_{K})\right)\,/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}\,\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">By construction, the restriction <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\lambda)|_{U\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Rees</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\lambda)|_{U\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> is canonically isomorphic to the <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the restriction to <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times 0</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle which admits a reduction of structure group to <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We can extend the structure group from <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> in order to obtain a <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\,\lambda)\times^{P_{\lambda}}G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Rees</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.297em">,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\,\lambda)\times^{P_{\lambda}}G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, the restriction <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\,\lambda)\times^{P_{\lambda}}G_{K}|_{U\times_{K}1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Rees</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.297em">,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><msub><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\,\lambda)\times^{P_{\lambda}}G_{K}|_{U\times_{K}1}</annotation></semantics></math> is canonically isomorphic to the original <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.14</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is split. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected scheme of finite type over <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle on <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Then every <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{K}\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}|_{U\times_{K}1}\cong\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}|_{U\times_{K}1}\cong\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\,\lambda)\times^{P_{\lambda}}G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Rees</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.297em">,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\,\lambda)\times^{P_{\lambda}}G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:\mathbb{G}_{m}\rightarrow G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:\mathbb{G}_{m}\rightarrow G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a cocharacter that is uniquely determined up to conjugation.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a </span><math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-reduction of </span><math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In other words, all <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtrations of <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> arise from weighted parabolic reductions of <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hei17</a>, Lem. 1.13]</cite>. The lemma found there is stated for a connected smooth projective curve over <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, but the same argument applies to any connected scheme of finite type over <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is split. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf over <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a big open subset of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally-free. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle on <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm14" title="Proposition 3.14. ‣ 3.3. Filtrations of 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves in the split case ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a>, all <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtrations of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> arise from the Rees construction applied to a weighted parabolic reduction <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have an associated weighted parabolic reduction <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\rho_{*}(\lambda),\rho_{*}(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\rho_{*}(\lambda),\rho_{*}(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}))</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. The Rees construction applied to this weighted parabolic reduction yields a filtration <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. This induces a filtration <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the pushforward of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> under the open inclusion <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\hookrightarrow X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\hookrightarrow X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subsheaf of the reflexive hull <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{\vee\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo>∨</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∨</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{\vee\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we set <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m}\vcentcolon=\widetilde{\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m}}\cap\mathcal{F}^{\vee\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo>∨</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∨</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m}\vcentcolon=\widetilde{\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m}}\cap\mathcal{F}^{\vee\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows by construction that <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the properties in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 3.8. ‣ 3.2. Filtrations of 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>, so it is indeed a filtration of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The pair <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\lambda)\times^{P_{\lambda}}G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Rees</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\lambda)\times^{P_{\lambda}}G)</annotation></semantics></math> is an element in <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Filt</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, it follows from our discussion above that every element of <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Filt</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> arises this way.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.15</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf on <math id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. A weighted parabolic reduction of <math id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a pair <math id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>, where</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I7" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implicit in the notation we have the choice of (any) big open subset </span><math id="S3.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S3.I7.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{U}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is locally-free. We denote by </span><math id="S3.I7.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> the </span><math id="S3.I7.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-bundle on </span><math id="S3.I7.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> corresponding to </span><math id="S3.I7.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I7.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a weighted parabolic reduction of </span><math id="S3.I7.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I7.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The filtration <math id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{m}\vcentcolon=\widetilde{\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m}}\cap\mathcal{F}^{\vee\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo>∨</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∨</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{m}\vcentcolon=\widetilde{\mathcal{F}^{\prime}_{m}}\cap\mathcal{F}^{\vee\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> described in the discussion above is called the underlying filtration associated to the parabolic reduction <math id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that <math id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> is nondegenerate if <math id="S3.Thmthm15.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is not the trivial cocharacter.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We record the description of filtrations of <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves obtained from the discussion above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.16</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is split. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf on <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then every filtration in <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Filt</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Filt(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\lambda)\times^{P_{\lambda}}G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Rees</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\lambda)\times^{P_{\lambda}}G)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighted parabolic reduction of <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> with underlying associated filtration <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm16.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm16.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm16.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, the filtration <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\lambda)\times^{P_{\lambda}}G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Rees</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}},\Rees(\mathcal{G}_{\lambda},\lambda)\times^{P_{\lambda}}G)</annotation></semantics></math> is nondegenerate if and only if <math id="S3.Thmthm16.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> is nondegenerate.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We can also give a concrete description of graded points of the stack <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. A graded <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf over <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be a morphism <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[\,\Spec(K)/\mathbb{G}_{m}\,\right]\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.130em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[\,\Spec(K)/\mathbb{G}_{m}\,\right]\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. By unraveling the definitions, this notion admits the following concrete description. A graded <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf on <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, plus a <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-grading <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\overline{\mathcal{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\overline{\mathcal{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of the torsion-free sheaf <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> such that each summand <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is also torsion-free. We get a well-defined <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-action on the scheme of <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-reductions <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> and we impose the condition that the section <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant. Hence we can think of morphisms <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\left[\,\Spec(K)/\mathbb{G}_{m}\,\right]\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.130em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\left[\,\Spec(K)/\mathbb{G}_{m}\,\right]\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> concretely as a graded pair <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\overline{\mathcal{F}}_{m},\sigma\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\overline{\mathcal{F}}_{m},\sigma\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We end this section by explaining a concrete description of the associated graded of a filtration. Suppose that <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is split. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf on <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> be a filtration of <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm16" title="Proposition 3.16. ‣ 3.3. Filtrations of 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves in the split case ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a> <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> comes from a weighted parabolic reduction <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> with underlying associated filtration <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can restrict <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to the point <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in\Theta_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in\Theta_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> in order to obtain a graded <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f|_{[0/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}]}:\left[\,\Spec(K)/\mathbb{G}_{m}\,\right]\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.130em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f|_{[0/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}]}:\left[\,\Spec(K)/\mathbb{G}_{m}\,\right]\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. The underlying graded torsion-free sheaf of <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f|_{[0/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f|_{[0/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}]}</annotation></semantics></math> is the associated graded <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can describe the <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-reduction over <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f|_{[0/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f|_{[0/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}]}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}=L_{\lambda}\ltimes U_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}=L_{\lambda}\ltimes U_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Levi decomposition defined by <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Then over the big open subset <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle is the one obtained by applying extension of structure group under the composition <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}=L_{\lambda}\ltimes U_{\lambda}\twoheadrightarrow L_{\lambda}\hookrightarrow G_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}=L_{\lambda}\ltimes U_{\lambda}\twoheadrightarrow L_{\lambda}\hookrightarrow G_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span><math id="S4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-stratification on <math id="S4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we define a polynomial numerical invariant <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> on the stack <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. We prove that <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> induces a <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-stratification, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>, Defn. 2.1.2]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, Defn. 2.23]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>The numerical invariant</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define a family of line bundles <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, §2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the line bundle on <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> determined as follows. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\Aff_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\Aff_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:T\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:T\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism, represented by a <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{T}:X_{T}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T}:X_{T}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> denote the structure morphism. Then, we set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{*}\,M_{n}\vcentcolon=\det\,\left(R\pi_{T\,*}\left(\mathcal{F}(n)\right)\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.130em">det</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.352em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\,M_{n}\vcentcolon=\det\,\left(R\pi_{T\,*}\left(\mathcal{F}(n)\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, we define <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{d}\vcentcolon=\bigotimes_{i=0}^{d}M_{i}^{\otimes(-1)^{d+i}\binom{d}{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{d}\vcentcolon=\bigotimes_{i=0}^{d}M_{i}^{\otimes(-1)^{d+i}\binom{d}{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We set <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the rational line bundle given by</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{*}\,L_{n}\vcentcolon=f^{*}\,M_{n}\otimes(f^{*}\,b_{d})^{-\otimes\,\overline{p}_{\mathcal{F}}(n)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.352em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\,L_{n}\vcentcolon=f^{*}\,M_{n}\otimes(f^{*}\,b_{d})^{-\otimes\,\overline{p}_{\mathcal{F}}(n)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an element of the rational Picard group <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic(\Bun_{\rho}(X))\otimes\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic(\Bun_{\rho}(X))\otimes\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> of the stack. This is what we mean by rational line bundle. We will henceforth omit the adjective “rational” and refer to <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as a line bundle. All the arguments will apply to the rational Picard group in the same way as for the usual Picard group.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The line bundle <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the restriction under the forgetful map <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)\rightarrow\Coh^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)\rightarrow\Coh^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of the line bundle <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Coh^{tf}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Coh</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Coh^{tf}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, §2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we define a rational quadratic norm on graded <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, §2.5, §4.1]</cite> for more details on rational quadratic norms).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define the rational quadratic norm <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> on graded points of <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\left[\Spec(K)/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}\right]\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\left[\Spec(K)/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}\right]\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> be represented by a graded <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\overline{\mathcal{F}}_{m},\sigma\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\overline{\mathcal{F}}_{m},\sigma\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(g):=\sum_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}m^{2}\cdot\rk(\overline{\mathcal{F}}_{m})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(g):=\sum_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}m^{2}\cdot\rk(\overline{\mathcal{F}}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We define the polynomial numerical invariant <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> to be the one determined by the family of line bundles <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and the rational quadratic norm <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, as it was done for the case of pure sheaves in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, §4.1]</cite>. For our purposes, it is sufficient to know the value of the numerical invariant associated to a nondegenerate <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration. Let us describe this next.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\supset k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊃</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\supset k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field extension, and let <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf on <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> be a nondegenerate filtration of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 3.9. ‣ 3.2. Filtrations of 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 3. Principal 
        
          ρ
        
      -sheaves ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a> this amounts to a filtration <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the torsion-free sheaf <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying some extra compatibility conditions. The restriction <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f|_{\left[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}\right]}:{\left[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}\right]}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f|_{\left[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}\right]}:{\left[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}\right]}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> yields a graded <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf on <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose underlying torsion-free sheaf is given by the associated graded <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(f)</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(f)\vcentcolon=\frac{\wgt\left(L_{n}|_{0}\right)}{\sqrt{b(f|_{[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}]})}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">wt</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(f)\vcentcolon=\frac{\wgt\left(L_{n}|_{0}\right)}{\sqrt{b(f|_{[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}]})}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The same computation as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, §4.1]</cite> shows that <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial numerical invariant (valued in <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>) which admits a description in terms of reduced Hilbert polynomials. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{A_{d}}{d!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{A_{d}}{d!}</annotation></semantics></math> be the leading coefficient of the Hilbert polynomial <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the structure sheaf with respect to <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\supset k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊃</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\supset k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field extension. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf on <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose a nondegenerate filtration <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Theta_{K}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has underlying associated filtration <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(f)=\sqrt{A_{d}}\,\cdot\,\frac{\sum_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}m\cdot(\overline{p}_{\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}}-\overline{p}_{\mathcal{F}})\cdot\rk(\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1})}{\sqrt{\sum_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}m^{2}\cdot\rk(\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1})}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.352em" rspace="0.352em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(f)=\sqrt{A_{d}}\,\cdot\,\frac{\sum_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}m\cdot(\overline{p}_{\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1}}-\overline{p}_{\mathcal{F}})\cdot\rk(\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1})}{\sqrt{\sum_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}m^{2}\cdot\rk(\mathcal{F}_{m}/\mathcal{F}_{m+1})}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The value of the denominator <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{b(f|_{[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}]})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{b(f|_{[0/\mathbb{G}_{m}]})}</annotation></semantics></math> follows by definition. The numerator has been computed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, §4.1]</cite>. The extra factor <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{A_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{A_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> appears because we define the rank to be the dimension of the fiber of <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> at the generic point, as opposed to the rank defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, §2.3]</cite> using the leading term of the Hilbert polynomial.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Given a weighted parabolic reduction <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> of a <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F},\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F},\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(f)</annotation></semantics></math> the value of the numerical invariant for the <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-filtration obtained by applying the Rees construction to <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{m})_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the underlying associated filtration of <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,\mathcal{G}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>, then the same formula (<a href="#S4.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ Proposition 4.4. ‣ 4.1. The numerical invariant ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) applies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Affine Grassmannians for <math id="S4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The setup for this subsection is as follows. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a quasicompact <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of torsion-free sheaves of rank <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that we are given an effective Cartier divisor <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\hookrightarrow X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\hookrightarrow X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> that is flat over <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=X_{S}\setminus D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=X_{S}\setminus D</annotation></semantics></math> (note that this is not a big open subset). The paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>]</cite> defines a notion of affine Grassmannian <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We recall the definition and some properties proven in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, §3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the functor from <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aff_{S}^{\,\,op}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aff_{S}^{\,\,op}</annotation></semantics></math> to sets given as follows. For every affine scheme <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aff_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aff_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, the set <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of equivalence classes of pairs <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E},\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E},\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, where</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a family of torsion-free sheaves on </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a morphism </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta:\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\rightarrow\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta:\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\rightarrow\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that the restriction </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta|_{Q_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta|_{Q_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Two such pairs <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E}_{1},\theta_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E}_{1},\theta_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E}_{2},\theta_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E}_{2},\theta_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are considered equivalent if there is an isomorphism <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{1}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{E}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{1}\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathcal{E}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> that identifies the morphisms <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> be the open immersion <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:Q\hookrightarrow X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:Q\hookrightarrow X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\Aff_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\Aff_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E},\theta)\in\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E},\theta)\in\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. The unit <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{E}\rightarrow j_{T\,*}\,j_{T}^{*}\,\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.352em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}\rightarrow j_{T\,*}\,j_{T}^{*}\,\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is a monomorphism. Hence we can use the isomorphism <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j_{T*}(j_{T})^{*}\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{T*}(j_{T})^{*}\theta</annotation></semantics></math> in order to view <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> as a subsheaf of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j_{T\,*}\,j_{T}^{*}\,\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.352em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{T\,*}\,j_{T}^{*}\,\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we see that <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is automatically injective, and we have <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\subset\mathcal{E}\subset j_{T\,*}\,j_{T}^{*}\,\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.352em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\subset\mathcal{E}\subset j_{T\,*}\,j_{T}^{*}\,\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is an increasing sequence of sheaves</p>
<table id="S4.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\subset\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}(D_{T})\subset\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}(2D_{T})\subset\cdots\subset\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}(nD_{T})\subset\cdots" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊂</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\subset\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}(D_{T})\subset\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}(2D_{T})\subset\cdots\subset\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}(nD_{T})\subset\cdots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We use this to define a truncated version of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X,D,\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X,D,\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive integer. <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr^{\leq N}_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr^{\leq N}_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subfunctor of <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined as follows. For all <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aff_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aff_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, we set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr^{\leq N}_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}(T)\;:=\;\left\{\begin{matrix}\;\;\;\text{pairs $(\mathcal{E},\theta)$ in $\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}(T)$ such that}\;\;\;\\
\text{$\mathcal{E}\subset\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}(ND_{T})$}\end{matrix}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.498em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">pairs </mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> in </mtext><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> such that</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr^{\leq N}_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}(T)\;:=\;\left\{\begin{matrix}\;\;\;\text{pairs $(\mathcal{E},\theta)$ in $\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}(T)$ such that}\;\;\;\\
\text{$\mathcal{E}\subset\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}(ND_{T})$}\end{matrix}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any rational polynomial <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{Q}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{Q}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N,P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N,P}</annotation></semantics></math> the open and closed subfunctor of <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of points <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E},\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E},\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> has Hilbert polynomial <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> on every fiber.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, Prop. 3.13]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmthm7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}=\underset{N&gt;0}{\mathop{\mathchoice{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\displaystyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\textstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\scriptstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\scriptscriptstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}}\displaylimits}\;\Gr^{\leq{N}}_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtable align="bottom1" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>colim</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>→</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">&gt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}=\underset{N&gt;0}{\mathop{\mathchoice{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\displaystyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\textstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\scriptstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\scriptscriptstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}}\displaylimits}\;\Gr^{\leq{N}}_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> as presheaves on <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aff_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aff_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N}=\bigsqcup_{P\in\mathbb{Q}[n]}\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N,P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N}=\bigsqcup_{P\in\mathbb{Q}[n]}\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N,P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N,P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N,P}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a projective scheme of finite presentation over <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. This induces a presentation of <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> as an ind-projective ind-scheme over <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose now that we have a section <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:Q\rightarrow\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:Q\rightarrow\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> defined over <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. We use this datum to define a version of the affine Grassmannian for <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The affine Grassmannian <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is the functor from <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\Aff_{S}\right)^{op}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\Aff_{S}\right)^{op}</annotation></semantics></math> into sets defined as follows. For each affine scheme <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aff_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aff_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of equivalence classes of the following data</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-sheaf </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E},\zeta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E},\zeta)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A morphism </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta:\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\rightarrow\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta:\mathcal{F}|_{X_{T}}\rightarrow\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that the restriction </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta|_{Q_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta|_{Q_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an isomorphism. Moreover, we require that the following diagram induced by the isomorphism </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta|_{Q_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta|_{Q_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> commutes.</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.Ex22.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="79.64" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-37.19px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 181.1 79.64" width="181.1"><g transform="translate(0,79.64) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(90.55,0) translate(0,37.19)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -90.55 -28.17)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 74.39)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 15.44)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(23.04,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -17.08 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.42em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.15" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="19.66"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Q_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Q_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 46.08 0) translate(84.48,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -44.58 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.39em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="74.65"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Red_{G}(\mathcal{E}|_{Q_{T}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Red_{G}(\mathcal{E}|_{Q_{T}})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 65.37)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 46.28 0) translate(84.29,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -44.39 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.37em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="74.26"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F}|_{Q_{T}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F}|_{Q_{T}})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -44.19 25.21 L -11.35 25.21"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -11.07 25.21)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -39.57 32.2)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.53em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.27em;font-size:10pt;" height="11" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.26)" width="24.15"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\zeta|_{Q_{T}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">ζ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\zeta|_{Q_{T}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -44.19 14.39 L 13.12 -12.22"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.90697 -0.4212 0.4212 0.90697 13.37 -12.34)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -44.07 -9.55)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.84em;--ltx-fo-height:0.53em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.27em;font-size:10pt;" height="11" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.26)" width="25.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\sigma|_{Q_{T}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\sigma|_{Q_{T}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.39998pt" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 44.96 -18.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.67em;--ltx-fo-height:0.71em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.89" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.89)" width="36.91"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xrightarrow{\;\;\sim\;\;}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xrightarrow{\;\;\sim\;\;}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Two elements above are considered equivalent if there is an isomorphism of <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves that is compatible with the morphism <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">There is a forgetful morphism <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}}\rightarrow\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}}\rightarrow\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E},\zeta,\theta)\mapsto(\mathcal{E},\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E},\zeta,\theta)\mapsto(\mathcal{E},\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The forgetful morphism witnesses <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a closed sub ind-scheme of <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Before proving Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 4.9. ‣ 4.2. Affine Grassmannians for 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a>, we first state a couple of lemmas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.10</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\longrightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\longrightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper morphism of finite presentation. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Suppose that <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is finitely presented and <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-flat. Fix a morphism <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:\mathcal{G}\rightarrow\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:\mathcal{G}\rightarrow\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> be the functor from <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes to sets that sends a <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{\sigma}(T)\vcentcolon=\;\left\{\begin{matrix}\text{morphisms $g:T\rightarrow S$ such that}\\
\text{$(\text{id}_{Y}\times_{S}g)^{*}\sigma=0$}\end{matrix}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.498em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">morphisms </mtext><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> such that</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">id</mtext><mi>Y</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\sigma}(T)\vcentcolon=\;\left\{\begin{matrix}\text{morphisms $g:T\rightarrow S$ such that}\\
\text{$(\text{id}_{Y}\times_{S}g)^{*}\sigma=0$}\end{matrix}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a closed subscheme of <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta23</a>, <a href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08K6" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Tag 08K6</a>]</cite>, there is a separated <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing morphisms <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}\to\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}\to\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. The two morphisms <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> yield two sections <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\sigma},f_{0}:S\rightarrow\Hom(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\sigma},f_{0}:S\rightarrow\Hom(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>. The functor <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by the locus where these two morphisms <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\sigma},f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\sigma},f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> agree. This is a closed subscheme of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, because <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{F})\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{F})\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math> is separated.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. Suppose that <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:Y\rightarrow X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:Y\rightarrow X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is an affine morphism of finite presentation. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:Q\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:Q\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a section of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> defined over the open complement <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=X_{S}\backslash D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=X_{S}\backslash D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.11</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define <math id="S4.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sect^{Y}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sect^{Y}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the functor from <math id="S4.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Aff_{S})^{op}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Aff_{S})^{op}</annotation></semantics></math> to sets given as follows. For <math id="S4.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\Aff_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\Aff_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{T}:Q_{T}\rightarrow Y_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{T}:Q_{T}\rightarrow Y_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}:Y_{T}\rightarrow X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}:Y_{T}\rightarrow X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the base-changes. We define</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Sect^{Y}_{s}(T)\;\vcentcolon=\;\left\{\begin{matrix}\;\;\;\text{sections $f:X_{T}\rightarrow Y_{T}$ of $p_{T}$}\;\;\;\\
\;\;\text{such that $f|_{Q_{T}}=s_{T}$}\;\;\;\end{matrix}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.498em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">sections </mtext><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> of </mtext><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">such that </mtext><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sect^{Y}_{s}(T)\;\vcentcolon=\;\left\{\begin{matrix}\;\;\;\text{sections $f:X_{T}\rightarrow Y_{T}$ of $p_{T}$}\;\;\;\\
\;\;\text{such that $f|_{Q_{T}}=s_{T}$}\;\;\;\end{matrix}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.12</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The functor <math id="S4.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sect^{Y}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sect^{Y}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a closed subscheme of <math id="S4.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">After passing to an affine open cover, we can assume that <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\Spec(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\Spec(R)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since everything is of finite presentation, we can use standard approximation results to reduce to the case when <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian. So <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian, and <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}(\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}(\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math> for some finitely generated <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can choose a finite open cover <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and a finite set of sections on each <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that generate <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}|_{V_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}|_{V_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{V_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{V_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta23</a>, <a href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PF" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Tag 01PF</a>]</cite> there exists a coherent subsheaf <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\subset\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\subset\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> that contains all of these sections. Serre vanishing implies that exists some positive integers <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N,m</annotation></semantics></math> and a surjective map of sheaves <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}^{\oplus m}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{F}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}^{\oplus m}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{F}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>. Twisting by <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}(-N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}(-N)</annotation></semantics></math>, we get a surjection <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}(-N)^{\oplus m}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}(-N)^{\oplus m}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. This induces a surjective map of <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym^{\bullet}\left(\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}(-N)^{\oplus m}\right)\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym^{\bullet}\left(\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}(-N)^{\oplus m}\right)\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. If we set <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}\vcentcolon=\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}(-N)^{\oplus m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}\vcentcolon=\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}(-N)^{\oplus m}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have a closed immersion <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:Y\hookrightarrow\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:Y\hookrightarrow\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us denote by <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}:Q\rightarrow\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}:Q\rightarrow\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> the composition of <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:Q\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:Q\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> with the closed immersion <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a natural map of functors <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sect^{Y}_{s}\rightarrow\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="1.140em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sect^{Y}_{s}\rightarrow\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> given by composition with <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The lemma is implied by the following two claims:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The functor <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="1.140em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a closed subscheme of <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sect^{Y}_{s}\rightarrow\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="1.140em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sect^{Y}_{s}\rightarrow\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed immersion.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">For claim (i), note that <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is just an element of <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(Q,\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(Q,\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{*}\,j^{*}\,\mathcal{E}=\underset{m\in\mathbb{Z}}{\mathop{\mathchoice{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\displaystyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\textstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\scriptstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\scriptscriptstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}}\displaylimits}\;\mathcal{E}(mD)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtable align="bottom1" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>colim</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"></mtd></mtr></mtable><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℤ</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}\,j^{*}\,\mathcal{E}=\underset{m\in\mathbb{Z}}{\mathop{\mathchoice{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\displaystyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\textstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\scriptstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\scriptscriptstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}}\displaylimits}\;\mathcal{E}(mD)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a positive integer <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> extends to a section <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\Gamma(X_{S},\mathcal{E}(MD))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\Gamma(X_{S},\mathcal{E}(MD))</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the composition <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\sigma}:\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}\xrightarrow{\sigma}\mathcal{E}(MD)\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{E}(MD)\,/\,\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜎</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.130em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\sigma}:\mathcal{O}_{X_{S}}\xrightarrow{\sigma}\mathcal{E}(MD)\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{E}(MD)\,/\,\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. The functor <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="1.140em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be seen to be isomorphic to the functor <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\overline{\sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\overline{\sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as in Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 4.10. ‣ 4.2. Affine Grassmannians for 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a>. The coherent sheaf <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(MD)\,/\,\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.130em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(MD)\,/\,\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-flat by the slicing criterion for flatness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta23</a>, <a href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/046Y" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Tag 046Y</a>]</cite>. Hence, Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 4.10. ‣ 4.2. Affine Grassmannians for 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a> implies that <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\overline{\sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\overline{\sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a closed subscheme of <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now proceed to prove claim (ii). Let <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. For our purposes, we can assume that <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is affine and Noetherian. Choose <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\rightarrow\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="1.140em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\rightarrow\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> represented by a section <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> of the structure morphism <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\Spec}_{X_{T}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}_{T}\right)\rightarrow X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\Spec}_{X_{T}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}_{T}\right)\rightarrow X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. We want to show that the fiber product <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}\times_{\Sect^{Y}_{s}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="1.140em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sect</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}\times_{\Sect^{Y}_{s}}T</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a closed subscheme of <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. For any scheme <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}\times_{\Sect^{Y}_{s}}T\,(W)=\left\{\begin{matrix}\text{morphisms $h:W\rightarrow T$ such that}\\
\;\;\text{$(\text{id}_{X_{T}}\times h)^{*}f:X_{W}\rightarrow\underline{\Spec}_{X_{W}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}_{W}\right)$}\;\;\\
\;\;\text{factors through $Y_{W}$}\;\;\end{matrix}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="1.140em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sect</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext>morphisms </mtext><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext> such that</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>id</mtext><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext>factors through </mtext><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}\times_{\Sect^{Y}_{s}}T\,(W)=\left\{\begin{matrix}\text{morphisms $h:W\rightarrow T$ such that}\\
\;\;\text{$(\text{id}_{X_{T}}\times h)^{*}f:X_{W}\rightarrow\underline{\Spec}_{X_{W}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}_{W}\right)$}\;\;\\
\;\;\text{factors through $Y_{W}$}\;\;\end{matrix}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">Define <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> to be the fiber product</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.Ex26.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="70.54" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-35.27px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 193.6 70.54" width="193.6"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,70.54) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(96.8,0) translate(0,35.27)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -96.8 -26.74)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 70.54)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.52)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 4.32 0) translate(18.47,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.51 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.81em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="11.19"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces B}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 79.47 0) translate(56.72,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -17.55 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.54em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="21.27"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces X_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 62.01)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(22.79,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -16.83 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.43em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="19.83"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 45.58 0) translate(90.61,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -51.45 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="89.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\underline{\Spec}_{X_{T}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}_{T}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\underline{\Spec}_{X_{T}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}_{T}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -74.01 15.41 L -74.01 -11.39"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -74.01 -11.67)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.39998pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -25.02 20.75)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.45" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.69)" width="10.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hookrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hookrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 39.39 13.33 L 39.39 -10.47"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 39.39 -10.74)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 42.65 -1.26)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -50.94 -23.28 L -18.84 -23.28"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -18.57 -23.28)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -49.44 -18.63)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.14em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.1em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.12" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="29.64"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\iota\times\text{id}_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">ι</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mtext mathsize="0.700em">id</mtext><mi mathsize="0.710em">T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\iota\times\text{id}_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p11.3.1" class="ltx_text">The morphism <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:B\hookrightarrow X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:B\hookrightarrow X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed immersion. For any scheme <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>-points of the fiber product can be alternatively described by</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}\times_{\Sect^{Y}_{s}}T\,(W)=\left\{\begin{matrix}\text{morphisms $h:W\rightarrow T$ such that}\\
\;\;\text{$(\text{id}_{X_{T}}\times h)^{*}m:B_{W}\hookrightarrow X_{W}$}\;\text{is an isomorphism}\end{matrix}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="1.140em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sect</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext>morphisms </mtext><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext> such that</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>id</mtext><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mtext>is an isomorphism</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X_{S}}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}\times_{\Sect^{Y}_{s}}T\,(W)=\left\{\begin{matrix}\text{morphisms $h:W\rightarrow T$ such that}\\
\;\;\text{$(\text{id}_{X_{T}}\times h)^{*}m:B_{W}\hookrightarrow X_{W}$}\;\text{is an isomorphism}\end{matrix}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p11.4.1" class="ltx_text">Let us denote by <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:\mathcal{J}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:\mathcal{J}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{X_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> the the inclusion of the <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaf of ideals <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the closed subscheme <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\hookrightarrow X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\hookrightarrow X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The fiber product <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}\times_{\Sect^{Y}_{s}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi mathsize="1.140em">Spec</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Sect</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sect^{\underline{\Spec}_{X}\left(\Sym^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\right)}_{s^{\prime}}\times_{\Sect^{Y}_{s}}T</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence it is represented by a closed subscheme of <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 4.10. ‣ 4.2. Affine Grassmannians for 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 4.9. ‣ 4.2. Affine Grassmannians for 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\Aff_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Aff</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\Aff_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\rightarrow\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\rightarrow\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism represented by a pair <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E},\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E},\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Definition <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Definition 4.8. ‣ 4.2. Affine Grassmannians for 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> the restriction <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta|_{Q_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta|_{Q_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> over the open subset <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. This yields an identification <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})|_{Q_{T}}\xrightarrow{\sim}\Red_{G}(\mathcal{E})|_{Q_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})|_{Q_{T}}\xrightarrow{\sim}\Red_{G}(\mathcal{E})|_{Q_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have a section <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> as follows</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:Q_{T}\xrightarrow{\sigma|_{Q_{T}}}\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})|_{Q_{T}}\xrightarrow{\sim}\Red_{G}(\mathcal{E})|_{Q_{T}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mover><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:Q_{T}\xrightarrow{\sigma|_{Q_{T}}}\Red_{G}(\mathcal{F})|_{Q_{T}}\xrightarrow{\sim}\Red_{G}(\mathcal{E})|_{Q_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">After unraveling the definitions, it can be seen that the fiber product <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}\times_{\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathsize="1.140em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}\times_{\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}}T</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally isomorphic to <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sect^{\Red_{G}(\mathcal{E})}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Sect</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="1.140em">Red</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sect^{\Red_{G}(\mathcal{E})}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm12" title="Lemma 4.12. ‣ 4.2. Affine Grassmannians for 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.12</span></a>, this is represented by a closed subscheme of <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{Q}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{Q}[n]</annotation></semantics></math> be a rational polynomial. For each <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> we define the subfunctors</p>
<table id="S4.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}^{\leq N}\vcentcolon=\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}\cap\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N}\;\;\;\text{and}\;\;\;\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}^{\leq N,P}\vcentcolon=\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}\cap\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N,P}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">∩</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0.827em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">∩</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}^{\leq N}\vcentcolon=\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}\cap\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N}\;\;\;\text{and}\;\;\;\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}^{\leq N,P}\vcentcolon=\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}\cap\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N,P}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p13.2" class="ltx_p">Both of these are closed sub ind-schemes of <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>. Propositions <a href="#S4.Thmthm7" title="Proposition 4.7 ([, Prop. 3.13]). ‣ 4.2. Affine Grassmannians for 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 4.9. ‣ 4.2. Affine Grassmannians for 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a> imply that <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}=\underset{N&gt;0}{\mathop{\mathchoice{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\displaystyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\textstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\scriptstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\scriptscriptstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}}\displaylimits}\;\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}^{\leq N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtable align="bottom1" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>colim</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"></mtd></mtr></mtable><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">&gt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}=\underset{N&gt;0}{\mathop{\mathchoice{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\displaystyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\textstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\scriptstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}{\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hfil$\scriptscriptstyle\operator@font colim$\hfil\cr\kern 1.5pt\cr\rightarrow\textstyle\cr\kern-1.0pt\cr\cr}}}}\displaylimits}\;\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}^{\leq N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore each <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}^{\leq N,P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}^{\leq N,P}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a scheme that is projective and of finite presentation over <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">There is a natural forgetful morphism <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}\rightarrow\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> that takes a point <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E},\zeta,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E},\zeta,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> and forgets the isomorphism <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. We can use this map to pullback the line bundle <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will abuse notation and denote this restriction by <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_coroll">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.13</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{Q}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{Q}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>. There exists some integer <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\gg 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\gg 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq m</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}^{\vee}|_{\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}^{\leq N,P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="1.140em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}^{\vee}|_{\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F},\sigma}^{\leq N,P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-ample.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows from Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 4.9. ‣ 4.2. Affine Grassmannians for 
          
            ρ
          
        -sheaves ‣ 4. 
        
          Θ
        
      -stratification on 
        
          
            
              
              Bun
              ρ
            
            
              
              
                (
                X
                ) ‣ The moduli stack of principal 
      
        ρ
      
    -sheaves and Gieseker-Harder-Narasimhan filtrations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, Prop. 3.19]</cite>, which states that <math id="S4.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> is eventually ample on <math id="S4.SS2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N,P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">Gr</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{X_{S},D,\mathcal{F}}^{\leq N,P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Monotonicity of the numerical invariant</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection we prove that the invariant <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bun_{\rho}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">Bun</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bun_{\rho}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-monotone and strictly <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-monotone. We recall what these notions mean, see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL14</a>, §5]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HLHJ21</a>, §2.5]</cite> for more details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We need some setup first. Let <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a complete discrete valuation ring over <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose a uniformizer <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\Theta_{R}}\vcentcolon=\mathbb{A}^{1}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\Theta_{R}}\vcentcolon=\mathbb{A}^{1}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-action that gives <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> weight <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{R}=\left[\,\mathbb{A}^{1}_{R}/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}\,\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.130em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{R}=\left[\,\mathbb{A}^{1}_{R}/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}\,\right]</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{1}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{1}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a unique <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant closed point cut out by the ideal <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>. We will denote this point by <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\overline{ST}_{R}}\vcentcolon=\Spec\left(\,R[t,s]/(st-\pi)\,\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.130em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.130em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\overline{ST}_{R}}\vcentcolon=\Spec\left(\,R[t,s]/(st-\pi)\,\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. We equip <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\overline{ST}_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\overline{ST}_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-action that gives <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> weight <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> weight <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The isomorphism class of the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\overline{ST}_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\overline{ST}_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the choice of uniformizer <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{ST}_{R}\vcentcolon=[\,Y_{\overline{ST}_{R}}/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}\,]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.130em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{ST}_{R}\vcentcolon=[\,Y_{\overline{ST}_{R}}/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}\,]</annotation></semantics></math>. We will denote by <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math> the unique <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-fixed closed point in <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\overline{ST}_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\overline{ST}_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have that <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math> is cut out by the ideal <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For any field <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> and integer <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{\kappa}[a]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{\kappa}[a]</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}_{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}_{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with the <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-action determined by the equation <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t\cdot[x:y]=[t^{-a}x:y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\cdot[x:y]=[t^{-a}x:y]</annotation></semantics></math>. We set <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0=[0:1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=[0:1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\infty=[1:0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty=[1:0]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.14</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Monotonicity)<span id="S4.Thmthm14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A polynomial numerical invariant <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> on a stack <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>-monotone (resp. strictly <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-monotone) if the following condition holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be any complete discrete valuation ring and set <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{ST}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{ST}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>). Choose a map <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:\mathfrak{X}\setminus(0,0)\rightarrow\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔛</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:\mathfrak{X}\setminus(0,0)\rightarrow\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, after maybe replacing <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> with a finite DVR extension, there exists a reduced and irreducible <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant scheme <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> with maps <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Sigma\rightarrow Y_{\mathfrak{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>𝔛</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Sigma\rightarrow Y_{\mathfrak{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\varphi}:\left[\Sigma/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}\right]\rightarrow\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\varphi}:\left[\Sigma/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}\right]\rightarrow\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(M1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The map </span><math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is proper, </span><math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant and its restriction over </span><math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\mathfrak{X}}\setminus(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>𝔛</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\mathfrak{X}}\setminus(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> induces an isomorphism </span><math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\,\Sigma_{Y_{\mathfrak{X}}\setminus(0,0)}\xrightarrow{\sim}Y_{\mathfrak{X}}\setminus(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.408em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>𝔛</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>𝔛</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\,\Sigma_{Y_{\mathfrak{X}}\setminus(0,0)}\xrightarrow{\sim}Y_{\mathfrak{X}}\setminus(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(M2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following diagram commutes</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.Ex30.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="75.16" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-37.73px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 207.04 75.16" width="207.04"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,75.16) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(103.52,0) translate(0,37.73)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -103.52 -28.9)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 74.85)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 15.44)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(66.99,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -61.03 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:7.77em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.36em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="107.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\left[\left(\Sigma_{Y_{\mathfrak{X}}\setminus(0,0)}\right)/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>𝔛</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.352em">/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\left[\left(\Sigma_{Y_{\mathfrak{X}}\setminus(0,0)}\right)/\,\mathbb{G}_{m}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 66.33)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 26.29 0) translate(40.7,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -34.74 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.97em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="54.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathfrak{X}\setminus(0,0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔛</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathfrak{X}\setminus(0,0)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 133.98 0) translate(53.5,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -13.6 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.92em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="12.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -4.06 11.72 L 63.62 -16.86"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.92123 -0.38902 0.38902 0.92123 63.88 -16.97)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 33.29 0.58)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0.72em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.99" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.99)" width="7.69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\widetilde{\varphi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.700em">φ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\widetilde{\varphi}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -36.53 11.72 L -36.53 -12.63"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -36.53 -12.91)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -46.27 -3.15)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 4.44 -25.44 L 63.58 -25.44"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 63.85 -25.44)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 30.65 -32.87)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.53em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="7.28"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
